  var ANSWERS = [
    /* round 9: in-depth questions the probe set found missing */
    ["what is comb filtering|why does it sound phasey where two speakers overlap|comb filter|hollow phasey sound between speakers|swooshy sound", "<p><strong>Comb filtering</strong> is what you hear when the same sound arrives from two places a few milliseconds apart — two speakers covering the same seat, two mics on one voice, or a mic and its own reflection. Some frequencies add and others cancel, so it sounds hollow, thin or \"swooshy\", and it changes as you move your head.</p><ul><li>Speakers: keep overlaps small (aim each box at its own area) and time-align fills and delays to the mains (§6.8).</li><li>Mics: one mic per source where you can, the 3:1 rule when you can't, and mute every mic that isn't in use (§4.6).</li></ul>", "s6-8 s4-6"],
    ["how do i check polarity without a polarity tester|check polarity by ear|is one speaker out of polarity|how to tell if a box is reversed|polarity check without a checker", "<p>By ear, with two identical boxes and the same signal:</p><ol><li>Low level, pink noise or a bass-heavy track, everything else muted.</li><li>Play both boxes and walk the line between them. In polarity, the low end gets <em>fuller</em> as you reach the middle. If one is reversed, the middle sounds thin, with a hole in the bass.</li><li>If it sounds reversed: swap that box's speaker cable for a known-good one first (a wrongly wired cable is the usual cause), then check the amp channel and the plan.</li><li>Subs the same way: weak bass where two subs overlap is the clue.</li></ol><p>A polarity checker or a measurement app (phase view in Open Sound Meter) confirms it. Don't change polarity settings on someone else's system — report what you heard.</p>", "s11-9 s6-8 s8-11"],
    ["difference between dual channel and mix top/sub|dual channel vs mix top sub|when to use mix top/sub or dual channel|dual channel or mix top sub", "<p><strong>Dual Channel</strong>: each amp channel drives its own box from its own NL4 output (A, B, C or D), each with its own setup. Any 2- or 4-core cable works because the channel is on both pin pairs.</p><p><strong>Mix TOP/SUB</strong>: a pair of channels (A/B or C/D) feeds a top <em>and</em> a sub down one 4-core cable from the centre output — top on pins 1±, sub on pins 2±. The cable goes to the sub and links on to the top.</p><p>Use Dual Channel when each box gets its own cable; Mix TOP/SUB to run one cable to a top-and-sub stack. The plan says which.</p>", "s6-17 s3-12"],
    ["how do i stop projector fan noise getting into the lectern mic|lectern mic picking up noise|fan noise in the mic|projector noise in lectern|air con noise in mic", "<p>Fix the pickup before the desk:</p><ol><li>Get the mic closer to the mouth (a gooseneck angled at the presenter, about a hand's width away) — the closer the mic, the quieter everything else is.</li><li>Point it away from the noise, and move the projector or fan if it sits on or beside the lectern.</li><li>High-pass the channel around 100–150 Hz to remove rumble.</li><li>A gate only for a mic used rarely — it can chop the start of words.</li></ol>", "s16-11 s4-3"],
    ["presenter sounds muffled|lav mic sounds muffled|headset sounds dull|presenter mic sounds boomy|speech sounds muddy", "<p>It's usually the mic, not the PA:</p><ul><li>A lav buried under clothing, on the wrong side, or rubbing; a headset boom too far back; a lectern mic aimed at the chest. Fix the position first.</li><li>Then EQ: high-pass around 100–150 Hz, cut mud around 200–400 Hz if it's boomy, and only then a small lift for clarity.</li></ul><p>Quick test: play music through the PA. If music sounds bright and only the mic is dull, it's the mic or its position.</p>", "s16-11 s10-11"],
    ["what is a good level for speech|how loud should speech be|speech level at a conference|how loud for a presentation", "<p>Comfortably above the room noise everywhere, not loud. Walk the room — the back and the edges matter as much as the mix position. If people at the back strain to hear, raise it or add fills; if the front rows wince, it's too loud or the PA is aimed wrong.</p><p>Turning a quiet presenter up is where feedback starts: get the mic closer (or switch to a headset) before reaching for the fader. Stay inside any venue noise limit.</p>", "s16-11 s6-10"],
    ["which setup for an e12 used as a wedge|can i use an e-series box as a wedge|d&b speaker as a monitor|using a top as a wedge", "<p>Use the setup the plan gives for that box — some d&b boxes have a separate setup for monitor use, and it's in the amp's setup list if it exists. If the plan doesn't say, ask before choosing one.</p><p>d&b's dedicated wedges are the M-Series (M2, M4, M6, MAX2). Whatever box is used, ring it out before the show.</p>", "s6-20 s15-8"],
    /* core audio */
    ["what is gain|gain vs volume|difference between gain and fader|what does the gain knob do|trim vs gain", "<p><strong>Gain</strong> (or trim) sets how hard the signal hits the desk's input — it's about getting a healthy, clean level in, not about loudness. The <strong>fader</strong> then decides how much of that channel goes into the mix.</p><p>Set gain first with the source playing at show level, then mix on faders. Turning gain up to make something louder in the mix is the classic beginner mistake — it eats headroom and brings feedback closer.</p>", "s2-4 s2-5"],
    ["what is gain structure|how do i set gain structure|gain staging|what is headroom|how much headroom", "<p><strong>Gain structure</strong> is keeping the signal at a sensible level at every stage of the chain, so nothing clips and nothing sits down in the noise.</p><ol><li>Set each input's gain so normal peaks land around the desk's nominal level, with room above for the loud moments.</li><li>Faders near their marked 0 (unity) point for the loudest sources.</li><li>Master at or near 0.</li><li>System (amps, processor) set by the system tech so the desk's full output doesn't drive the PA past its limits.</li></ol><p><strong>Headroom</strong> is the gap between where you normally run and where it clips — on a digital desk, leave a generous margin (often 15–20 dB) for surprises.</p>", "s2-5 s18-4"],
    ["mic level vs line level|what is line level|what are signal levels|speaker level|instrument level|dbu vs dbv", "<p>There are four worlds of signal level:</p><ul><li><strong>Mic level</strong> — tiny, needs lots of preamp gain.</li><li><strong>Instrument level</strong> — guitars and basses; high impedance, needs a DI.</li><li><strong>Line level</strong> — desks, players, processors. Pro gear runs around +4 dBu, consumer gear around −10 dBV.</li><li><strong>Speaker level</strong> — the amp output: real voltage and current. Never plug it into anything else.</li></ul><p>Mixing worlds up gives you either distortion (too hot) or noise (too quiet).</p>", "s2-2"],
    ["balanced vs unbalanced|what is a balanced cable|why are xlr cables balanced|why does balanced reject noise", "<p>A <strong>balanced</strong> line carries the signal twice — once normal, once polarity-flipped — plus a screen. At the far end one copy is flipped back and the two are added: the signal doubles and any interference picked up equally on both wires cancels out.</p><p>That's why XLR and TRS runs can go long distances quietly, while unbalanced (TS jack, RCA phono) should be kept short.</p>", "s2-3"],
    ["what is phantom power|what is 48v|do dynamic mics need phantom|can phantom damage a mic|ribbon mic phantom", "<p><strong>Phantom power</strong> is +48 V DC sent up a balanced mic cable (on pins 2 and 3) to power condenser mics and active DI boxes.</p><ul><li>Dynamic mics don't need it and a correctly wired balanced dynamic isn't harmed by it.</li><li>Mute the channel (or the PA) before switching it on or off, and before plugging — the pop can be huge.</li><li>Be careful with ribbon mics and anything unbalanced or faulty: ask before applying it.</li></ul>", "s2-6"],
    ["what is impedance|does impedance matter|ohms speaker impedance|what does 8 ohm mean", "<p><strong>Impedance</strong> is how much a device resists the signal flowing into it. For mics and line gear you mostly don't need to think about it — pro gear is designed to match.</p><p>Where it bites: instrument pickups (high impedance — use a DI), and loudspeakers, where too many boxes in parallel on one amp channel drops the load too low and the amp complains. On d&amp;b systems, check the boxes-per-channel limits in §6.20.</p>", "s2-7 s6-20"],
    ["polarity vs phase|what is polarity|what is phase|polarity flip|phase button", "<p><strong>Polarity</strong> is simply which way up the waveform is — the desk's Ø button flips it, instantly, at every frequency. <strong>Phase</strong> is about timing: a delay shifts different frequencies by different amounts.</p><p>Two mics on one source, or two speakers covering one seat, that are out of polarity or misaligned in time will cancel — thin, hollow sound. Flip polarity to check; fix timing with delay.</p>", "s2-8"],
    ["what is word clock|what is clocking|why does digital audio need a clock|sample rate mismatch|clicks digital", "<p>Every digital device samples audio at a fixed rate (usually 48 or 96 kHz). When devices pass digital audio between them, they must all agree on exactly when each sample happens — one is the <strong>clock leader</strong>, the rest follow.</p><p>Two leaders, or mismatched sample rates, give clicks, pops, dropouts or silence. On Dante the network elects a leader automatically, but check it's the one the system design expects.</p>", "s2-9 s8-2"],
    ["what is a decibel|what is db|how much louder is 6db|what is 3db|10db louder", "<p>A <strong>decibel</strong> is a ratio, not a fixed amount. Useful rules of thumb:</p><ul><li><strong>+3 dB</strong> — double the power; a just-noticeable change.</li><li><strong>+6 dB</strong> — double the voltage (or SPL pressure); clearly louder.</li><li><strong>+10 dB</strong> — sounds roughly twice as loud.</li><li>Every doubling of distance from a small source: about <strong>−6 dB</strong> outdoors.</li></ul><p>The calculators in Support do the maths for you.</p>", "s2-10 support-calc"],
    ["what frequency is|frequency chart|what lives where|where is the boom|where is mud|where is sibilance|frequency crib", "<p>A rough map:</p><ul><li><strong>Below 60 Hz</strong> — sub, kick weight; felt more than heard.</li><li><strong>100–250 Hz</strong> — warmth; too much is boom and mud.</li><li><strong>250–500 Hz</strong> — boxiness.</li><li><strong>1–4 kHz</strong> — presence and intelligibility; too much is harsh. Most feedback lives here on vocals.</li><li><strong>5–8 kHz</strong> — sibilance (s and t sounds).</li><li><strong>10 kHz and up</strong> — air.</li></ul>", "s2-11 s16-2"],
    ["speed of sound|how much delay per metre|delay calculation|how many milliseconds per metre", "<p>Sound travels about <strong>344 m/s</strong> in air, so roughly <strong>2.9 ms per metre</strong>. A delay speaker 30 m back needs roughly 87 ms to line up with the main PA, plus a little extra so the main system is heard first.</p><p>Use the distance &amp; delay calculator in Support for the exact figure.</p>", "s6-8 support-calc"],
    ["inverse square law|how much quieter further away|spl drop distance|6db per doubling", "<p>For a small (point) source in the open, level falls by about <strong>6 dB every time the distance doubles</strong>. A tall line array behaves differently up close — nearer 3 dB per doubling in its near field — which is part of why it throws further.</p><p>Indoors, reflections stop the level falling as fast. The SPL calculator in Support works the free-field figure out for you.</p>", "s6-6 support-calc"],

    /* cables and connectors */
    ["xlr pinout|which pin is hot on xlr|xlr pin 1 2 3|what are the pins on an xlr", "<p>Standard XLR audio: <strong>pin 1</strong> screen (ground), <strong>pin 2</strong> hot (+), <strong>pin 3</strong> cold (−). Pin 2 hot is the international standard; very old American gear sometimes used pin 3 hot.</p>", "s3-1"],
    ["trs vs ts|what is a trs jack|stereo jack vs balanced jack|what is trrs", "<p>A <strong>TS</strong> jack (tip, sleeve) is unbalanced mono — guitars, some keyboards. A <strong>TRS</strong> jack (tip, ring, sleeve) is either a balanced mono line <em>or</em> an unbalanced stereo pair (headphones, insert cables) — same plug, different job, so check what the socket expects. <strong>TRRS</strong> is the phone headset plug: stereo plus a mic.</p>", "s3-2"],
    ["what is speakon|why speakon not jack for speakers|speakon vs jack|speaker cable vs instrument cable", "<p><strong>speakON</strong> is the locking connector for speaker-level signals. Speaker cable carries real power, so it uses thick, unscreened cores and a connector that locks and can't be touched when live.</p><p>Never use an instrument jack cable to drive a speaker — the thin screened cable overheats and the jack can short the amp.</p>", "s3-3"],
    ["nl4 vs nlt4|what is an nlt4|difference between nl4 and nlt4|what does nlt4 mean|nl4fx nlt4fx", "<p>On the shelf they usually mean the same thing: a <strong>four-pole speakON cable</strong>. The letters are Neutrik part numbers for the connector:</p><ul><li><strong>NL4FX / NL4FC</strong> — the standard plastic-bodied cable connector.</li><li><strong>NLT4FX</strong> — the STX heavy-duty, all-metal version. Same four pins, same socket, tougher and higher-rated: what touring and rental stock uses.</li><li><strong>NLT4MX</strong> — a male cable end, so one cable can plug straight into another (extensions).</li></ul><p>Either fits the same NL4MP socket on the amp or box.</p>", "s3-12"],
    ["nl2 vs nl4|what is an nl2|can i use nl2 in nl4 socket|two pole speakon", "<p>An <strong>NL2</strong> is a two-pole speakON: it fits a four-pole socket but only connects <strong>pins 1+/1−</strong>. That's the classic trap — anything that needed pins 2 (a sub in Mix TOP/SUB, the HF of a 2-way active box, a link to the next box) goes silent. Use 4-core NL4/NLT4 wherever pins 2 are in play.</p>", "s3-12 s6-17"],
    ["nl4 pinout|what do nl4 pins carry|pins 1 and 2 speakon|what is on pin 2 of speakon|speakon wiring", "<p>A four-pole speakON has two pairs:</p><ul><li><strong>1+ / 1−</strong> — the first (or only) channel: tops and full-range boxes, the LF in 2-way active, channel A or C on d&amp;b.</li><li><strong>2+ / 2−</strong> — a second channel or a link through: subs in Mix TOP/SUB, the HF in 2-way active, channel B or D.</li></ul><p>So one 4-core cable can carry two amp channels. A 2-core cable on NL4 ends only carries pins 1.</p>", "s3-12 s6-17"],
    ["how do i lock a speakon|speakon wont lock|how to connect speakon|how to release speakon", "<ol><li><strong>Mute</strong> the amp channel first.</li><li>Line up the key and push fully home — don't force it.</li><li>Twist clockwise about a quarter turn until it clicks. No click means not locked.</li><li>Tug-test gently on the connector body.</li><li>To release: pull back the latch tab, twist anticlockwise, pull the body straight out — never the cable.</li></ol>", "s3-12"],
    ["what is nl8|nl8 cable|eight pole speakon|speaker multicore", "<p><strong>NL8</strong> is the eight-pole speakON — four amp channels down one cable. It's used for speaker multicores (for example from an amp rack to a breakout near a hang). It doesn't fit NL4 sockets; you need a breakout or a matching amp output.</p>", "s3-12 s3-5"],
    ["what is a multicore|what is a stagebox|what is a loom|snake cable", "<p>A <strong>multicore</strong> (snake) carries many signals in one cable. A <strong>stagebox</strong> is the box on stage you plug mics into — analogue, or (now usually) a digital stagebox that converts to a network or digital link back to the desk. A <strong>loom</strong> is a bundle of separate cables taped or sleeved together. Label both ends, always.</p>", "s3-5"],
    ["cat5 vs cat6|which ethercon cable|ethercon|network cable for audio|how long can a cat cable be", "<p>Network cable is now core audio infrastructure. Use shielded Cat5e or Cat6 with <strong>etherCON</strong> (locking) ends for touring. A single copper run is limited to <strong>100 m</strong>; beyond that, use a switch in the middle or fibre. Treat it gently — kinks and crushed cable cause intermittent drops.</p>", "s3-6"],
    ["what is aes50|what is madi|what is aes3|digital audio formats", "<p>The main digital transports:</p><ul><li><strong>AES3 (AES/EBU)</strong> — two channels on one 110 Ω XLR cable.</li><li><strong>MADI</strong> — up to 64 channels on coax or fibre.</li><li><strong>AES50</strong> — Midas/Behringer's point-to-point link on Cat cable.</li><li><strong>Dante</strong> — audio over standard IP networks; the most common.</li><li><strong>AVB / Milan</strong> — network audio with timing built into the switches; d&amp;b and L-Acoustics support Milan.</li></ul>", "s3-7 s8-2"],
    ["what is powercon|powercon true1|blue and grey powercon|can i unplug powercon live", "<p><strong>powerCON</strong> is the locking mains connector on amps and powered speakers. The older <strong>blue (in) / grey (out)</strong> type must not be connected or disconnected under load. <strong>TRUE1</strong> (black/yellow) is rated to break under load. Either way: turn the device off first where you can, and lock it — twist until it clicks.</p>", "s3-8"],
    ["16a vs 32a|what is a ceeform|blue ceeform|16 amp plug|32 amp plug|red ceeform", "<p>Industrial <strong>CEE form</strong> connectors are colour-coded: <strong>blue</strong> is single-phase 230 V (16 A, 32 A, 63 A), <strong>red</strong> is three-phase 400 V. A 16 A blue is the everyday one for small audio racks. Mains distribution — and anything three-phase — is the qualified electrician's or the power tech's job, not yours.</p>", "s3-8 s9-6 s12-15"],
    ["what is a di box|why use a di|active vs passive di|when do i need a di|di box", "<p>A <strong>DI box</strong> turns an unbalanced, high-impedance source (guitar, bass, keyboard, laptop) into a balanced mic-level signal the stagebox can take over a long run. It also isolates grounds.</p><ul><li><strong>Passive DI</strong> — no power, very robust; great for keys and hot sources.</li><li><strong>Active DI</strong> — needs phantom or a battery; better for passive pickups (bass, acoustic).</li></ul><p>The <strong>ground lift</strong> switch is the first thing to try on a hum.</p>", "s3-9 s12-10"],
    ["ground lift|what does ground lift do|is ground lift safe|hum from laptop", "<p>A <strong>ground lift</strong> on a DI disconnects the audio screen (pin 1) at one end to break a ground loop — the usual cure for hum from laptops, keyboards and video kit. It only lifts the <em>audio</em> screen; it's safe.</p><p><strong>Never</strong> remove the mains earth from anything (no cheater plugs, no cutting earth pins). That's dangerous and illegal.</p>", "s3-9 s12-3"],
    ["how to coil a cable|over under|how should i coil cables|cable care", "<p>Coil <strong>over–under</strong>: one loop normal, the next with a half twist the other way. The cable lies flat, doesn't kink, and throws out straight without tangling. Tie it with a velcro tie, label both ends, and never wrap it round your elbow.</p>", "s3-10"],
    ["how do cables fail|why do cables break|which cables fail most|cable fault", "<p>Cables mostly fail at the <strong>ends</strong>: a broken solder joint or core where the strain relief has given up, or a connector that's been stood on. Symptoms are crackle when the cable moves, intermittent drop-outs, or noise. Wiggle-test near each end; replace, don't bodge. Quarantine and label faulty cables so they don't go back on the shelf.</p>", "s3-11 s11-5"],

    /* microphones */
    ["dynamic vs condenser|what is a condenser mic|what is a dynamic mic|ribbon mic|types of microphone", "<ul><li><strong>Dynamic</strong> — robust, no power needed, handles high level: vocals on loud stages, guitar amps, drums (SM58, SM57).</li><li><strong>Condenser</strong> — needs phantom power, more detailed and sensitive: overheads, acoustic instruments, choirs, lecterns.</li><li><strong>Ribbon</strong> — smooth and fragile; careful with phantom and wind.</li></ul>", "s4-1"],
    ["cardioid vs supercardioid|polar pattern|what is cardioid|omni mic|figure of eight", "<p>A mic's <strong>polar pattern</strong> is where it listens.</p><ul><li><strong>Cardioid</strong> — front, rejects the back. Wedges go behind it.</li><li><strong>Supercardioid / hypercardioid</strong> — tighter at the front, with a small pick-up lobe directly behind, so wedges go off to either side of the back (roughly 120–130° from the front), not dead centre.</li><li><strong>Omni</strong> — everywhere; natural, but more feedback-prone.</li><li><strong>Figure-of-8</strong> — front and back, not the sides.</li></ul>", "s4-2"],
    ["proximity effect|why is the vocal boomy close|bass boost close mic", "<p><strong>Proximity effect</strong>: directional mics boost bass the closer the source gets. That's why a singer eating the mic sounds boomy, and why a high-pass filter on most vocal channels is standard. It's also a tool — a presenter can warm up their voice by moving closer.</p>", "s4-3"],
    ["where to put a mic|how to mic a guitar amp|how to mic a kick|how to mic drums|mic placement", "<p>Start points (then listen and move):</p><ul><li><strong>Guitar amp</strong> — a dynamic close to the grille, pointed at the speaker cone; centre is brighter, towards the edge is warmer.</li><li><strong>Kick</strong> — inside the port or just inside the drum; closer to the beater is more click.</li><li><strong>Snare</strong> — a few cm above the rim, angled at the centre, rejecting the hi-hat.</li><li><strong>Vocal</strong> — close, on-axis, with the wedge in the mic's dead zone.</li></ul><p>The placement crib sheet has the full set.</p>", "s4-5"],
    ["3 to 1 rule|three to one rule|two mics one source|xy vs ab|stereo mic technique", "<p>The <strong>3:1 rule</strong>: when two mics pick up different sources, keep the distance between the mics at least three times each mic's distance from its own source — it keeps spill low enough to avoid nasty comb-filtering. For stereo pairs: <strong>XY</strong> (coincident) is mono-safe; <strong>AB</strong> (spaced) is wider but less mono-compatible.</p>", "s4-6"],
    ["how to look after mics|mic hygiene|cleaning mic grilles|dropped mic", "<p>Hold mics by the body, not the grille; don't blow into them; store them in their case or a padded drawer. Clean vocal mic grilles regularly (remove and wash the grille and foam, dry fully). A dropped condenser should be tested before the show, not discovered during it.</p>", "s4-8"],
    ["what mic for lectern|lectern mic|gooseneck|conference mic|mic for speeches", "<p>For a lectern, a <strong>gooseneck condenser</strong> (cardioid or supercardioid) aimed at the speaker's mouth from 20–40 cm. Presenters move around, so keep it a little further away rather than very close, high-pass it, and ring it out with the room PA before people arrive. A headset or lavalier radio mic suits a presenter who walks.</p>", "s4-4 s10-2"],

    /* wireless & RF */
    ["what is channel 38|do i need a licence for radio mics|ofcom licence radio mic|uk radio mic licence|pmse licence", "<p>In the UK, most professional radio mics and IEMs need an <strong>Ofcom PMSE licence</strong>. <strong>Channel 38</strong> (606.5–613.5 MHz) is a UK-wide shared licence (£75 a year bought online, Nov 2026) — the everyday hire-stock band — but it's shared and unprotected, so you still scan and plan. Other UHF channels are coordinated per site and date. A small licence-exempt band (around 863–865 MHz) suits a few low-power mics. Check §5.4 and Ofcom for current details.</p>", "s5-4"],
    ["how to set up radio mics|frequency scan|how do i scan for frequencies|sync radio mic|radio mic setup", "<ol><li>Switch everything that will be on during the show on (LED walls, other radio systems).</li><li>Scan with the receivers or the coordination software.</li><li>Pick a coordinated set of frequencies (intermod-free), within your licence.</li><li>Sync each transmitter to its receiver (IR sync).</li><li>Walk test every mic in every spot it will be used.</li><li>Fresh batteries, label packs and receivers.</li></ol>", "s5-8 s11-6 s19-8"],
    ["what is intermod|intermodulation|why do frequencies need coordinating|frequency coordination", "<p>When several transmitters are on at once, they mix inside receivers and transmitters to create <strong>intermodulation products</strong> — ghost signals on new frequencies. If one lands on another mic's frequency, that mic gets noise or drop-outs. Coordination software (Wireless Workbench, Sennheiser WSM and similar) picks sets of frequencies where the products miss everything you're using.</p>", "s5-5"],
    ["antenna placement|where to put radio mic antennas|antenna distribution|paddle antenna|line of sight", "<p>Keep <strong>line of sight</strong> from transmitter to antenna, get antennas up above head height, away from metal and LED walls, and at least a quarter wavelength apart for diversity. Use the right antenna for the band, low-loss coax, and an antenna distribution system rather than daisy-chained whips. Most drop-outs are antenna problems, not mic problems.</p>", "s5-3 s5-9"],
    ["radio mic batteries|how long do batteries last|rechargeable radio mic|aa batteries radio mic", "<p>Start every show with <strong>fresh or fully charged</strong> batteries — never part-used ones. Use the type the system expects (alkaline AAs, or the manufacturer's rechargeable packs), note when they went in, and change between shows on long days. Battery status on the receiver is a guide, not a guarantee.</p>", "s5-6"],
    ["what is an iem|in ear monitor|how do iems work|iem setup", "<p><strong>IEMs</strong> (in-ear monitors) send each performer's monitor mix wirelessly (or wired) to earpieces. They cut stage volume and feedback and protect hearing — but they're radio too, so they need licensing, coordination and their own clean frequencies. Start the performer's volume low, and give them an ambient or crowd mic so they don't feel cut off.</p>", "s5-7 s15-2 s15-3"],
    ["radio mic dropping out|why does my radio mic drop out|rf dropouts|wireless keeps cutting out", "<p>Usually in this order: <strong>antennas</strong> (line of sight, placement, cables), <strong>interference</strong> (a new device, an LED wall, someone else on your frequency — rescan), <strong>batteries</strong>, then <strong>body-pack position</strong> (sweat, against skin, under metal). Watch the receiver's RF meter while the performer walks the dead spot.</p>", "s5-9 s12-11"],
    ["rf exposure|is rf dangerous|emf safety|radio mic health", "<p>Radio mic and IEM transmitters are very low power and well within exposure guidelines. Larger transmitters, broadcast links and antennas on site are a different matter — respect exclusion zones and signage, and don't stand in front of high-power antennas.</p>", "s5-10"],

    /* PA */
    ["what is a line array|line array vs point source|why line array|how does a line array work", "<p>A <strong>line array</strong> is a column of boxes that couple together into one tall source, so it throws sound a long way with even level front to back. A <strong>point source</strong> box spreads from a single point and drops off faster. Line arrays need prediction software, careful splay angles and proper rigging; point sources are simpler for smaller rooms.</p>", "s6-2 s6-6"],
    ["what is a sub|why do subs need their own amp|subwoofer setup|sub placement", "<p>A <strong>sub</strong> reproduces the lowest frequencies, usually below about 100 Hz. Subs are crossed over from the tops, often driven by their own amp channels or via Mix TOP/SUB on d&amp;b. Placement matters as much as the box: ground-stacked across the front, in an arc, or in a cardioid arrangement to reduce bass behind them.</p>", "s6-2 s6-6"],
    ["what is a cardioid sub|cardioid sub array|why is there bass on stage|reduce bass on stage", "<p>A <strong>cardioid sub array</strong> uses some subs facing (or delayed) backwards to cancel bass behind the array while keeping it in front — less boom on stage and at the desk end of a venue. It only works if the spacing, delay and polarity are exactly as designed, so don't change any of it without the system tech.</p>", "s6-6 s6-19"],
    ["what is an amp|what does an amplifier do|amp vs processor|what is dsp in an amp", "<p>An <strong>amplifier</strong> turns a line-level signal into speaker-level power. Modern touring amps like d&amp;b's also contain the <strong>processing</strong> (DSP): crossover, EQ, delay and limiters, set by a speaker-specific setup. That's why the right setup for the right box matters — the wrong one can sound bad or damage drivers.</p>", "s6-4 s6-16"],
    ["what is a limiter|why is my amp limiting|gr light|gain reduction|amp limiting", "<p>A <strong>limiter</strong> stops the signal going above a set level to protect the speakers. On a d&amp;b amp, a yellow <strong>GR</strong> (gain reduction) indicator means the limiter is working. Occasional flicker on peaks is normal; constant GR means the system is being driven too hard — turn down at the desk, or tell the system tech you need more boxes.</p>", "s6-4 s6-17"],
    ["what is system tuning|how is a pa tuned|time alignment|what is smaart", "<p><strong>System tuning</strong> is setting delays, levels and EQ so the PA sounds even and correct across the audience. It's done with a measurement mic and software (Smaart, or the free Open Sound Meter) comparing what goes in with what the mic hears. Tuning corrects the system's response — it doesn't fix the room, and it's the system tech's job on most shows.</p>", "s6-8 s8-3"],
    ["what is arraycalc|prediction software|what is easefocus|soundvision", "<p><strong>Prediction software</strong> models how a PA will cover a venue before it's rigged: ArrayCalc (d&amp;b), Soundvision (L-Acoustics), and similar from other makers. It produces the splay angles, rigging loads and amp setups. On d&amp;b jobs, the ArrayCalc file is the plan.</p>", "s6-7 s6-16"],
    ["how loud is too loud|spl limits|noise limits at events|dba limit|venue noise limit", "<p>Events often have <strong>sound level limits</strong> set by the licence or the local authority (for the neighbours) and the venue. Separately, the Noise at Work Regulations protect workers: action values at 80 and 85 dB(A) daily exposure. Your job: know the limit, have the meter running where the plan says, and tell the engineer early if you're getting close.</p>", "s6-10 s13-5"],
    ["what is a hearing loop|induction loop|t setting hearing aid|assistive listening", "<p>A <strong>hearing loop</strong> (induction loop) carries audio as a magnetic field round a room so hearing-aid users on the T setting hear it directly. It needs a clean feed (usually the speech mics, not the music), a correctly installed loop and a check with a loop listener. Infrared and radio systems are the alternatives.</p>", "s6-11 s19-15"],

    /* consoles */
    ["what is a channel strip|signal flow through desk|how does a mixing desk work", "<p>The <strong>channel strip</strong> is the path every input takes: gain → high-pass filter → gate → EQ → compressor → inserts → aux sends → pan → fader → routing to buses or the main mix. Learn one strip properly and you can find your way round any desk.</p>", "s7-1"],
    ["pfl vs afl|what is pfl|what is afl|solo on a desk|what does solo do", "<p><strong>PFL</strong> (pre-fade listen) solos the channel as it comes in, ignoring the fader — use it to set gain and check a signal. <strong>AFL</strong> (after-fade listen) solos it as it is in the mix, after the fader and pan. Solo only changes what you hear in headphones; it never changes the PA.</p>", "s7-6"],
    ["what is an aux send|pre fade vs post fade|monitor sends|aux pre or post", "<p>An <strong>aux send</strong> feeds a channel to a separate mix — monitors or effects. <strong>Pre-fade</strong> sends ignore the channel fader: use them for monitors, so your FOH moves don't change the band's wedges. <strong>Post-fade</strong> sends follow the fader: use them for reverb and effects.</p>", "s7-1 s15-1"],
    ["what is a dca|vca vs dca|what is a group bus|dca vs subgroup", "<p>A <strong>DCA</strong> (or VCA) is a remote control for the faders of several channels — no audio passes through it. A <strong>group / subgroup</strong> is an actual audio bus the channels are summed into, so you can process them together. Use DCAs for quick level control of sections (drums, vocals), groups when you want shared processing.</p>", "s7-3"],
    ["what is a scene|show file vs scene|recall safe|how do i save a show file|scene recall", "<p>A <strong>show file</strong> is everything about the desk's setup; a <strong>scene</strong> is a snapshot of settings you can recall. <strong>Recall safe</strong> protects chosen channels or parameters from being changed by a scene recall. Before touching anything: save a copy of the show file with the date in the name, onto a USB stick too.</p>", "s7-4"],
    ["what is an offline editor|practise a desk at home|desk software free|learn a console without the console", "<p>Most digital desks have a free <strong>offline editor</strong> for a computer — the same screens as the real desk. Use it to learn the layout, build and check show files before the job, and practise. Yamaha (DM7 Editor, CL/QL Editor), Allen &amp; Heath, DiGiCo and Midas all offer one.</p>", "s8-1 s18-2"],
    ["what is a gate|noise gate|expander|when to use a gate", "<p>A <strong>gate</strong> mutes (or turns down) a channel when the signal drops below a threshold — useful on toms and kick to cut spill. An <strong>expander</strong> is gentler: it turns quiet signals down by a ratio. Set the threshold just under the hits, and the release so tails aren't chopped.</p>", "s16-3"],
    ["what is a compressor|compressor settings|threshold ratio attack release|how to set a compressor", "<p>A <strong>compressor</strong> turns down a signal by a <strong>ratio</strong> when it goes over the <strong>threshold</strong>, evening out level. <strong>Attack</strong> is how fast it reacts; <strong>release</strong> how fast it lets go. Starting point for vocals: ratio 3:1, medium attack, release that recovers between phrases, 3–6 dB of gain reduction on the loud bits, then make-up gain.</p>", "s16-4 s7-5"],
    ["subtractive eq|how to eq|cut or boost|eq tips|how do i eq a vocal", "<p>Cut before you boost. Find what's wrong (boom, boxiness, harshness), cut it narrowly, and only then add a little of what's missing. High-pass everything that doesn't need low end. For a vocal: HPF around 80–120 Hz, cut mud around 200–400 Hz if needed, gentle presence around 2–5 kHz only if it isn't cutting through.</p>", "s16-1 s7-5"],
    ["how to build a mix|mixing from scratch|where to start mixing|mix order", "<p>Get every channel clean and gained first. Then build the foundation (kick and bass), add the rest of the drums, then the harmonic instruments, and the vocal on top — the vocal is usually the most important thing in the mix. Keep checking in the room, not just on headphones, and leave headroom for the show to grow.</p>", "s16-6"],
    ["what is talkback|talkback mic|how do i talk to stage|oscillator tone|pink noise", "<p><strong>Talkback</strong> is a mic at the desk routed to wedges, IEMs or comms so you can speak to the stage. The desk's <strong>oscillator</strong> sends test tone or pink noise to chosen outputs — useful for checking every speaker and line. Always start it at low level, and warn people before you send noise.</p>", "s7-6"],
    ["console gotchas|common desk mistakes|why is there no sound from desk|desk not outputting", "<p>The greatest hits: a channel or mix <strong>muted</strong>, a <strong>DCA or mute group</strong> down, a <strong>scene recall</strong> changed something, the <strong>patch</strong> doesn't route to the output you think, the master fader is down, or an output is in <strong>solo/PFL</strong> only. Follow the signal: input meter → channel → bus → output patch → physical output.</p>", "s7-7 s12-12"],
    /* Dante and networks */
    ["what is dante|how does dante work|dante explained|audio over network", "<p><strong>Dante</strong> sends many channels of audio over a standard IP network. Every device has transmit and receive channels; you connect them with <strong>Dante Controller</strong> (free software) by ticking the grid. One device becomes the clock leader. It's the network audio system you're most likely to meet, and Audinate's free certification is worth doing.</p>", "s8-2 s18-3"],
    ["dante primary secondary|dante redundancy|what is the secondary port|should i connect secondary to primary", "<p>Many Dante devices have a <strong>primary</strong> and a <strong>secondary</strong> port. In redundant mode they must go to <strong>two completely separate networks</strong> (separate switches, never linked). Plugging secondary into the same switch as primary, or linking the two networks, can break the whole system. If there's no secondary network, leave the secondary port empty.</p>", "s8-2 s12-14"],
    ["what is dante controller|how to patch in dante controller|dante routing|subscribe dante channels", "<p><strong>Dante Controller</strong> is the free app for routing: transmitters across the top, receivers down the side, tick where they cross to subscribe. Green tick = working, a warning symbol = a problem (often clock, sample rate or bandwidth). Name devices clearly and save a preset of the routing before the show.</p>", "s8-2"],
    ["what switch for dante|eee green ethernet dante|dante switch settings|qos dante|igmp snooping", "<p>Dante works on most decent gigabit managed switches, but they need setting up: turn <strong>EEE / Green Ethernet off</strong> (it causes clicks and dropouts), enable <strong>QoS</strong> so clock and audio get priority, and use <strong>IGMP snooping</strong> carefully if there's lots of multicast. Don't plug audio into a house or office network without the network owner's say-so.</p>", "s8-6 s12-14"],
    ["what ip address|dhcp vs static|link local|169.254|why cant i see the device on the network", "<p>Dante devices usually pick their own address: from a <strong>DHCP</strong> server if there is one, otherwise a <strong>link-local</strong> 169.254.x.x address. Your laptop has to be on the same network and addressing scheme to see them. If a device is missing: check the cable and switch port lights, that you're on the right network (and Wi-Fi is off), and the laptop's IP settings.</p>", "s8-6 s12-14"],
    ["what is vlan|what is a vlan|network basics for audio|what is a switch", "<p>A <strong>switch</strong> connects network devices together. A <strong>VLAN</strong> splits one physical switch into separate virtual networks — for example audio on one, lighting or control on another. Useful, but only change switch setups if you know the design; one wrong port can join networks that should stay apart.</p>", "s8-6"],
    ["what is r1|d&b r1|how to connect r1|r1 remote|amp not in r1", "<p><strong>R1</strong> is d&amp;b's remote control software: it shows and controls every amp — mutes, levels, setups, delays, temperatures, errors. Connect the laptop to the amp control network (Ethernet), open the project, and go online. If an amp is missing, check its network cable, IP settings and that you're on the right network.</p>", "s6-16 s6-17"],
    ["what is comms|what is clearcom|talkback system|what is an intercom|belt pack", "<p><strong>Comms</strong> (intercom) lets crew talk on channels via belt packs and headsets — Clear-Com, Riedel, Green-GO and others. Typical channels: production, sound, lighting, stage. Check every pack before doors, keep the mic flipped up or off when not talking, and never key a channel to chat.</p>", "s8-5 s11-8"],
    ["what is qlab|playback software|how to play music at an event|walk on music", "<p><strong>QLab</strong> (Mac) is the standard show-control and playback software for theatre and events: cues fire in order with one key. For simple events, a dedicated playback laptop or player with a backup copy is fine. Disable notifications, sleep and updates, and route through a proper DI or interface.</p>", "s8-4"],

    /* test before doors */
    ["system check vs line check|what is a line check|what is a system check|difference between line check and sound check", "<p>A <strong>system check</strong> proves the PA: every box, every amp channel, the right setup, levels and coverage. A <strong>line check</strong> proves every input: each mic or DI gets to the right desk channel cleanly. A <strong>soundcheck</strong> is the band playing so the engineer can mix. Do them in that order.</p>", "s11-1 s11-3"],
    ["how to do a line check|line check steps|line checking|line check without talkback", "<ol><li>Patch list in hand.</li><li>Phantom off until you need it, PA muted or low.</li><li>One input at a time: speak or scratch at the mic, watch the right channel meter move.</li><li>Check it's clean — no crackle, hum or distortion.</li><li>Tick the sheet; mark anything faulty and quarantine it.</li></ol><p>No talkback? Use a phone or a walkie and a second person, or PFL and headphones.</p>", "s11-3 s11-4 s19-5"],
    ["how to prove a d&b system|test the pa before doors|check every speaker|pre event test", "<p>Prove it one box at a time at low level with pink noise or music, against the plan: right box, right amp channel, right setup, no limiting, no errors in R1. Then the whole system together, then walk the room. The 15-step test is in Job Hub → System test, and the chat can run it with you.</p>", "s11-9 s6-18"],
    ["what is quarantine|faulty kit|what do i do with broken cable|label faulty equipment", "<p><strong>Quarantine</strong> means taking faulty kit out of use so it can't go back on the shelf: tape it, label it with the fault and date, and put it in the fault bin or bag. Tell whoever handles repairs. A cable that's ‘probably fine’ is the one that fails during the show.</p>", "s11-5"],
    ["how to walk test radio mics|walk test|wireless proving", "<p>With everything on that will be on during the show (LED walls, lighting, other radio), walk each radio mic to every spot it'll be used — including the far corners and behind the set — while someone watches the receiver RF and audio meters and listens. Note any drops and fix antenna placement before doors.</p>", "s11-6"],

    /* faults (knowledge-type) */
    ["what causes hum|ground loop|why is there a buzz|50hz hum|mains hum", "<p><strong>Hum</strong> (a steady 50 Hz tone and its harmonics) is usually a <strong>ground loop</strong>: two pieces of kit earthed at different points, joined by an audio cable. Fixes in order: find which input it's on, try the DI's ground lift, use a balanced connection or isolation transformer, keep audio cables away from dimmers and power. Never remove a mains earth.</p>", "s12-3"],
    ["how to fault find|fault finding method|troubleshooting method|how to find a fault", "<p>Work like a crew chief: <strong>define</strong> the symptom exactly, <strong>split</strong> the chain in half to find which side it's on, change <strong>one thing at a time</strong>, and use <strong>substitution</strong> — swap in a known-good part. Cheapest and most likely first. Write down what you've checked, so the handover makes sense.</p>", "s12-1 s12-4"],
    ["intermittent fault|fault comes and goes|crackle when moving cable|keeps cutting out", "<p>Intermittent faults are usually <strong>mechanical</strong>: a connector, a solder joint, a cable end. Wiggle-test each connection in the chain while listening (at low level), and watch for patterns — moving, heat, vibration, a time of day. Swap the suspect part rather than chasing it during a show.</p>", "s12-5 s3-11"],
    ["what is feedback|why does feedback happen|how to stop feedback|howl", "<p><strong>Feedback</strong> is a loop: the mic hears the speaker, it's amplified again, and it runs away at one frequency. Immediately: pull the fader (or the mix) down. Then: keep mics behind the PA, wedges in the mic's dead zone, mics closer to the source, fewer open mics, and notch the ringing frequency on the right output EQ.</p>", "s12-18 s15-8"],
    ["what to do when something breaks during show|first sixty seconds|fault during show", "<p>First sixty seconds: <strong>make it safe</strong> (mute if it's loud or dangerous), <strong>tell</strong> your lead in one sentence, <strong>work around</strong> it if there's an obvious spare (swap the mic, use another channel), and only then diagnose. Keep the show going; fix it properly in the next gap.</p>", "s12-8"],
    ["when should i ask for help|who do i tell|when to stop|escalate a fault", "<p>Stop and get help when it involves <strong>mains power, rigging or someone else's system</strong>, when anything is unsafe, when you've done the obvious checks and it's still broken, or when the fix needs a change you aren't authorised to make. Hand over the facts: the symptom, what changed, what you've checked, what you need.</p>", "s12-7"],
    ["how to keep a fault log|fault log|write up a fault|report a fault", "<p>A good fault note: <strong>what</strong> failed, <strong>when</strong>, <strong>which</strong> item (asset number), <strong>what you checked</strong>, and <strong>what you did</strong>. Put it on the item's label and in the job notes. The chat writes it for you at the end of any fault route.</p>", "s12-17"],

    /* safety and law */
    ["electricity at work regulations|am i allowed to touch power|what electrical work can i do|eawr", "<p>The <strong>Electricity at Work Regulations 1989</strong> require electrical systems to be safe and the people working on them to be competent. In practice: you can do user checks (visual inspection, plugging in kit that's been tested) but mains distribution, generators and anything three-phase are for the qualified person. If in doubt, don't.</p>", "s13-2 s12-15"],
    ["what is loler|what is puwer|working at height|ladder rules|rigging rules", "<p><strong>LOLER</strong> covers lifting equipment (hoists, rigging, truss) and needs it examined and used by competent people. <strong>PUWER</strong> covers all work equipment. The <strong>Work at Height Regulations</strong> cover ladders, towers and lifts. Don't climb, rig or fly anything you aren't trained and authorised for.</p>", "s13-3"],
    ["manual handling|how to lift a speaker|lifting heavy kit|two person lift", "<p>Plan the lift: weight, route, help. Lift close to your body with your legs, don't twist, and use two people (or a dolly or hoist) for anything heavy or awkward — a big speaker or amp rack is always at least two. Use the handles and the case wheels; it's not weak to ask.</p>", "s13-4"],
    ["noise at work|how loud before ear protection|hearing protection|ear plugs|80 85 db", "<p>The <strong>Control of Noise at Work Regulations 2005</strong> set action levels at <strong>80 dB(A)</strong> and <strong>85 dB(A)</strong> daily exposure — a loud gig blows past both. Carry decent filtered earplugs and wear them during soundcheck and loud sets, not just the show. Hearing damage is permanent and invisible until it's done.</p>", "s13-5"],
    ["what is rams|risk assessment|method statement|do i need to read the rams", "<p><strong>RAMS</strong> is the Risk Assessment and Method Statement: what could go wrong on this job, how it's controlled, and how the work will be done safely. Read it before the job, follow it on site, and say if what you're asked to do isn't in it or doesn't match reality.</p>", "s13-7"],
    ["what is cdm|cdm 2015 events|principal contractor event", "<p><strong>CDM 2015</strong> (Construction Design and Management) applies to building and taking down events, not just building sites. It means there's a principal contractor in charge of site safety, inductions, and rules everyone follows. For you: attend the induction, follow the site rules, wear the PPE asked for.</p>", "s13-6"],
    ["what is pat testing|does kit need pat test|pat test sticker|in service inspection", "<p>“PAT testing” isn't a legal requirement in itself — the law requires electrical equipment to be maintained safely. In practice hire companies inspect and test regularly and label kit. Before you use anything: a quick <strong>visual check</strong> — damaged cable, cracked plug, scorch marks, a missing or out-of-date label. Faulty means quarantine.</p>", "s13-8"],
    ["what ppe do i need|steel toe caps|hard hat|hi vis|ppe for events", "<p>Typical event PPE: <strong>safety boots</strong> (toe caps) always on load-in and out, <strong>hi-vis</strong> where vehicles move, <strong>hard hat</strong> when work is going on overhead, <strong>gloves</strong> for loading, and <strong>hearing protection</strong>. The site rules and RAMS say what's required.</p>", "s13-11"],
    ["riddor|accident reporting|near miss|someone got hurt", "<p>Report every accident and near miss to the site or company safety person — that's how things get fixed. <strong>RIDDOR</strong> requires the employer to report serious injuries, some dangerous occurrences and injuries that keep someone off work for more than seven days to the HSE. First, get first aid and make the area safe.</p>", "s13-13"],
    ["what is coshh|haze fluid|hazardous substances|chemicals on site", "<p><strong>COSHH</strong> covers hazardous substances: haze and smoke fluid, cleaning products, solvents, battery acid. Use them as the safety data sheet says, ventilate, and tell people with asthma before haze goes up. Don't decant chemicals into unlabelled bottles.</p>", "s13-14 s9-12"],
    ["working time regulations|how many hours can i work|48 hour week|opt out|rest break|break entitlement", "<p>The <strong>Working Time Regulations</strong> limit average working time to <strong>48 hours a week over 17 weeks</strong> (most event employers ask you to sign an opt-out, which is normal and can be withdrawn with notice). You're also entitled to <strong>11 hours' rest</strong> between working days, a <strong>20-minute break</strong> in a shift over six hours, and paid holiday.</p>", "s13-15"],
    ["working alone|lone working|early start alone|late finish safety", "<p>If you're working alone: someone must know where you are and when you'll be done, you check in at agreed times, and you don't do anything high-risk alone (rigging, heavy lifts, mains). Keep a charged phone and know the venue's emergency procedure.</p>", "s13-12"],
    ["fire exit|site induction|emergency procedure|what to do in a fire", "<p>At every venue: attend the induction, find the <strong>fire exits, assembly point and fire alarm sound</strong>, and never block exits or extinguishers with cases or cable. Know who the first aider is. If the alarm goes, leave — don't stay to save kit.</p>", "s13-10"],

    /* the job */
    ["what does an audio technician do|what is my role|what is a sound tech job|role on site", "<p>The audio technician builds, tests, runs and takes down the sound system so the engineer and the show can work. That means prep, cabling, system and line checks, solving faults, looking after kit, and being calm and reliable. Engineers mix; system techs own the PA design; you make it all work, and learn every part of it.</p>", "s1-1 s1-3"],
    ["who is who on site|production manager|stage manager|system tech|foh engineer", "<p>The usual chain: <strong>production manager</strong> (the whole show), <strong>stage manager</strong> (the stage and running order), <strong>FOH engineer</strong> (the audience mix), <strong>monitor engineer</strong> (the performers' mixes), <strong>system tech</strong> (the PA design and tuning), then technicians and crew. Know who you report to on each job.</p>", "s1-3"],
    ["what is advancing|advance a show|tech rider|what is a rider", "<p><strong>Advancing</strong> is confirming the details before the job: timings, access, power, the <strong>tech rider</strong> (what the act needs), the <strong>input list</strong> and stage plot, contacts and parking. Most on-the-day problems are things nobody advanced.</p>", "s1-7 s19-9"],
    ["what paperwork do i need|patch sheet|pick list|job sheet", "<p>The core paperwork: <strong>job sheet</strong> (times, place, contacts), <strong>pick list</strong> (the kit), <strong>stage plot</strong> and <strong>input/patch list</strong>, the system drawing, and the RAMS. Keep them on your phone and on paper; update them when things change, so the derig and the next job are easy.</p>", "s1-5 s8-8"],
    ["site etiquette|unwritten rules|how to behave on site|crew etiquette", "<p>Be early, ask before touching anyone else's kit, don't stand on the stage during a performance or walk through the audience's sightlines, keep your phone away during the show, label everything, and help with the boring jobs. Say what you don't know — it's far better than guessing.</p>", "s1-6"],
    ["warehouse prep|how to prep a job|prep checklist|what to check before leaving warehouse", "<p>Prep from the pick list: every item <strong>counted, tested and labelled</strong>. Test every cable, power up every amp and desk, check radio mics and spares, pack the fault kit. Load the van in reverse order of unloading. The warehouse prep checklist in §19.2 has the full list.</p>", "s19-2"],
    ["load in order|how to load in|load-in|build order", "<p>A typical order: power and distribution first (by the right people), then PA position and rigging, amps and speaker cables, stage boxes and multicores, desk and network, then stage mics and monitors. Test as you go, not all at the end. The load-in checklist in §19.4 follows that.</p>", "s19-4"],
    ["derig|load out|pack down|after the show", "<p>Derig safely and tidily: wait for the all-clear, power down in the right order (amps before the desk), coil cables properly, count everything back against the pick list, and put faulty kit in quarantine with a label. Leave the venue as you found it.</p>", "s19-7 s19-3"],

    /* monitors and IEMs */
    ["how to ring out a wedge|ringing out|ring out monitors|eq a wedge", "<p>With the mic where the performer will be: raise the wedge level slowly until it just starts to ring, find that frequency (an RTA helps), cut it narrowly on the wedge's output EQ by a few dB, and carry on. Two or three cuts, not twenty — after that, move the mic or the wedge instead.</p>", "s15-8"],
    ["wedge vs iem|wedges or in ears|should we use iems", "<p><strong>Wedges</strong> are simple, robust, and performers hear the room — but add stage volume and feedback risk. <strong>IEMs</strong> give a cleaner, quieter stage and protect hearing, but need radio coordination, careful mixes and an ambient mic so performers don't feel cut off. Many shows mix both.</p>", "s15-2"],
    ["how to build an iem mix|iem mix|in ear mix|monitor mix for singer", "<p>Start with the performer's own voice or instrument, then add only what they ask for, in the order they ask. Keep it low to start, give them a stereo mix if you can, add a little reverb and an ambient mic for the room, and keep checking in — monitor mixes are a conversation.</p>", "s15-3 s15-7"],
    ["what is a click track|click in ears|drummer click|backing track", "<p>A <strong>click track</strong> is a metronome (and often cues) sent only to the drummer's or band's ears to keep time with backing tracks or video. It must never go to the PA. Route it carefully, label it clearly, and check it's on the right mixes during line check.</p>", "s15-10"],

    /* recording and streaming */
    ["how to record a gig|multitrack recording|record from the desk|virtual soundcheck", "<p>Most digital desks can send every channel over USB, Dante or another network to a computer for a <strong>multitrack recording</strong>. Set the sample rate to match the desk, record pre-fader (so the recording isn't affected by your mix moves), check the drive has space, and run a backup recording if it matters. Played back into the desk, it's a <strong>virtual soundcheck</strong>.</p>", "s14-2 s14-3 s14-7"],
    ["sample rate and bit depth|48k or 96k|24 bit|what format to record", "<p>For live recording: <strong>48 kHz, 24-bit WAV</strong> is the safe default (match the desk's sample rate). 96 kHz doubles the file size with little gain for most jobs. Never record to MP3 as the master.</p>", "s14-4"],
    ["broadcast feed|press feed|what is a submix for broadcast", "<p>A <strong>broadcast or stream feed</strong> needs its own mix — what sounds right in the room (with the PA doing the heavy lifting) is wrong on a stream. Build a separate submix with more vocals and room mics, check the level and loudness the broadcaster wants, and agree the format and connector before the day.</p>", "s14-5 s14-8"],
    ["can i record the show|recording rights|consent to record|is it legal to record", "<p>Only record a show if the people with the rights — the act, the promoter, the venue — have <strong>agreed</strong>. Recording for the engineer's virtual soundcheck still needs permission. Never share or post anything you recorded without written consent.</p>", "s14-11"],

    /* career */
    ["how do i become a sound engineer|career path|how to progress|what next in my career", "<p>Common routes: technician → system tech or monitor engineer → FOH engineer, or into production management, broadcast, install or touring. Progress comes from doing every job properly, saying yes to new responsibilities, learning the desks and software you meet (offline editors are free), and keeping evidence of your work.</p>", "s17-1 s17-6"],
    ["freelance|how to go freelance|self employed sound|day rate", "<p>Freelancing basics: register as self-employed with HMRC, keep records of income and expenses, agree your rate and payment terms before the job, get your own liability insurance, and invoice promptly. Build a network of companies and engineers who know your work.</p>", "s17-2"],
    ["what training should i do|certifications|dante certification|courses for audio", "<p>Worth having: <strong>Dante certification</strong> (free, online), manufacturer training for the kit you use (d&amp;b, Yamaha, Shure), an IPAF or PASMA ticket if your work needs it, first aid, and any console training you can get. Part 18 lists free courses.</p>", "s17-4 s18-3"],
    ["what kit should i buy|own tools|what should be in my bag|tools for sound tech", "<p>First buys: a good <strong>torch</strong>, <strong>multitool</strong>, <strong>cable tester</strong>, gaffa and electrical tape, Sharpies, a few adapters, <strong>filtered earplugs</strong>, headphones, and a USB stick for show files. A small multimeter later. See §19.17 for the full bag list.</p>", "s17-5 s19-17"],

    /* events */
    ["corporate event audio|conference audio|what changes at corporate events|corporate av", "<p>Corporate work is about <strong>speech</strong> being clear, every time, and the client never noticing the sound. Expect lectern and radio mics, playback, video feeds, hybrid links and a tight schedule. Ring out every mic, have spare radio mics on, check every presenter's mic before they walk on, and keep the volume comfortable.</p>", "s10-2 s19-18"],
    ["theatre sound|musicals|working in theatre|theatre show day", "<p>Theatre is precise and repeatable: radio mics on every cast member, a cue-based show, QLab playback, and strict show-day routines. Mic checks, battery changes and the same order every time. See §10.4 and the theatre show-day checklist.</p>", "s10-4 s19-19"],
    ["hybrid meeting audio|zoom audio|teams call audio|echo on video call|mix minus", "<p>For hybrid events, the remote people need the room's speech and the room needs to hear them — without sending them their own voices back (echo). That's a <strong>mix-minus</strong>: send the call everything except the call's own return. Check both directions with a real test call before the start.</p>", "s10-8"],
    ["wedding audio|party audio|small function|dj setup", "<p>Small functions are about simple, reliable kit and people skills: a speech mic that works first time, background music at a sensible level, the DJ connected cleanly (DI if needed), and a plan for the first dance and speeches. Do a quick check with the couple or organiser.</p>", "s10-9"],
    ["outdoor gig|rain|weather|working outside|outdoor event audio", "<p>Outdoors: cover the desk and electronics, keep connectors off the ground, watch the wind (speaker stacks and flown kit), plan for cold batteries and condensation, and know the site's weather-stop rules. Sound behaves differently outside — no walls to help, and wind moves it around.</p>", "s6-14 s19-14"],
    ["festival|festival audio|changeover|festival stage", "<p>Festivals mean fast changeovers, shared stages and strict curfews. Prep line systems and stage boxes in advance, label everything, use the patch the stage manager gives you, and keep the changeover calm and in order. Noise limits are policed.</p>", "s10-10"],
    /* researched additions (Nov 2026) */
    ["what is a dm7|yamaha dm7|dm7 vs dm7 compact|how many channels dm7|dm7 inputs", "<p>The <strong>Yamaha DM7</strong> has 120 input channels, 48 mixes, 12 matrices and two stereo buses, 32 local inputs and 16 outputs, and 144×144 Dante built in at up to 96 kHz. The <strong>DM7 Compact</strong> has the same bus structure with 72 inputs, one fader bay and 16 local in and out.</p>", "s7-8"],
    ["how do i set up a dm7|dm7 setup|dm7 show file|load show file dm7", "<p>Save what's on the desk to USB first, then load or initialise the show file, check Dante (devices, sample rate, clock leader), patch inputs and outputs, set up mixes, Recall Safe what must not change, line check, then store a scene and save again. Full order in §7.9 — or say “set up a DM7” and I'll walk you through it.</p>", "s7-9 s7-8"],
    ["dm7 recall safe|dm7 focus recall|scene changed my channel dm7|what is focus recall", "<p><strong>Recall Safe</strong> protects chosen channels or parameters from every scene recall. <strong>Focus Recall</strong> sets, per scene, what that scene is allowed to change. If a scene changed something it shouldn't, one of these is set wrong — tell the engineer before recalling anything else.</p>", "s7-8 s7-4"],
    ["dm7 two screens select|unlink sel keys dm7|dm7 surface settings", "<p>On the full DM7, each bay can have its own selected channel: Setup → User Setup → Surface, and unlink the SEL keys. Most engineers prefer it, so the two screens show two different channels.</p>", "s7-8"],
    ["dm7 rio gain|ha remote|who controls the rio gain|gain compensation", "<p>A DM7 sets the gain and phantom of Yamaha Rio stage boxes over Dante (<strong>HA remote</strong>). On a shared FOH and monitor system, one person's gain change moves everyone's levels — so only the preamp owner changes them. Ask who owns the preamps before the job.</p>", "s7-8"],
    ["dm7 recording|record on a dm7|dm7 usb channels|dm7 multitrack", "<p>The DM7's USB carries 18 channels in and out — fine for stereo or a small recording. For a full multitrack, use <strong>Dante Virtual Soundcard</strong> on a computer (up to 64 channels at 48 kHz) and record pre-fader.</p>", "s7-9 s14-7"],
    ["what is the dugan automixer|automixer|automatic mixer for speech|panel mics feedback", "<p>An <strong>automixer</strong> (the DM7 has Dan Dugan's built in) automatically shares gain between speech mics, so the total stays steady however many people talk. Open panel mics don't add up to feedback and room noise, and nobody has to ride faders for every word.</p>", "s7-8 s10-11"],
    ["digico vs yamaha|which desk is which|compare desks|which desk", "<p>The channel strip is the same on every desk; the words and scene systems change. Yamaha uses <strong>scenes</strong> with Recall Safe and Focus; DiGiCo uses <strong>snapshots</strong> with recall scope; Allen &amp; Heath uses <strong>scenes</strong> with recall filters and safes. On any desk you don't know, save the show file first and find the solo-clear and the main mute.</p>", "s7-10"],
    ["shure wireless workbench|how to use wireless workbench|wwb deploy|ulxd setup|sennheiser ewdx setup", "<p>With everything that will be on during the show switched on: scan, add your devices' frequencies, <strong>Analyze</strong>, <strong>Calculate</strong>, then <strong>Assign and Deploy</strong> to the networked receivers. Batteries in, sync each transmitter, set gain, label, walk test. Sennheiser's WSM or Control Cockpit do the same job.</p>", "s5-11 s5-8"],
    ["how much is a radio mic licence|channel 38 licence cost|ofcom fee|channel 70 licence free|licence exempt radio mic", "<p>As of November 2026: the UK shared licence for <strong>Channel 38</strong> (606.5–613.5 MHz) is <strong>£75 a year or £135 for two years</strong> bought on Ofcom's website (£85 / £155 otherwise). <strong>Channel 70</strong> (863.1–864.9 MHz) is licence-exempt — free, but crowded. Bigger shows use coordinated site licences. Check ofcom.org.uk for current fees.</p>", "s5-12 s5-4"],
    ["l-acoustics|la12x|la network manager|kara preset|l acoustics vs d&b|what is soundvision", "<p>L-Acoustics works like d&amp;b with different names: amplified controllers (LA4X, LA12X, LA7.16) instead of D-series amps, <strong>presets</strong> instead of setups, <strong>LA Network Manager</strong> instead of R1, and <strong>Soundvision</strong> instead of ArrayCalc. Preset suffixes: <strong>_FI</strong> for fills, <strong>_MO</strong> for monitors.</p>", "s6-21"],
    ["what is milan|milan vs dante|avb switch|does d&b use milan|milan network", "<p><strong>Milan</strong> is network audio on AVB: the switches themselves keep time and reserve bandwidth, so there's no QoS or IGMP setup — but they <strong>must be AVB/Milan-capable</strong>. d&amp;b D40/D90, L-Acoustics and Meyer support it. Redundancy needs two physically separate networks. Don't mix Milan and Dante on the same switches unless the design says so.</p>", "s8-10"],
    ["dante controller step by step|how to route dante|dante clock leader|preferred leader|dante preset", "<p>Laptop on the audio network by cable (Wi-Fi off), open Dante Controller, check every device is there, one clock leader (set Preferred Leader on that device only), sample rates match, then tick transmit-meets-receive in the grid. Rename devices <em>before</em> routing, and save a preset of the routing.</p>", "s8-9"],
    ["open sound meter|free measurement software|how to measure a pa|transfer function|smaart free alternative", "<p><strong>Open Sound Meter</strong> is free measurement software. You need a two-input interface and a measurement mic: input 1 the mic, input 2 the pink noise looped back as reference. Use the delay finder, then read magnitude, phase and coherence. Measuring is for learning; changing the system's EQ is the system tech's call.</p>", "s8-11 s6-8"],
    ["corporate presenter mic|how to mic a presenter|lavalier placement|lapel mic placement|lav placement|where to clip a lapel mic|headset or lav", "<p>Lavalier on the sternum (about a hand's width below the chin), on the side they turn to, cable taped so it can't rustle. Headsets for movers and quiet talkers; handhelds for Q&amp;A. Fresh battery, gain set so they don't clip, muted until they walk on, and rung out with the room PA before doors.</p>", "s10-11 s5-11"],
    ["martyns law|martyn's law|terrorism protection of premises act|event security law|invacuation", "<p><strong>Martyn's Law</strong> (Terrorism (Protection of Premises) Act 2025) is expected to come into force in <strong>spring 2027</strong>. Venues and events of 200–799 people need public protection procedures (evacuation, invacuation, lockdown, communication); 800 or more also need protective measures. It's the venue's duty — yours is to know the signals and keep the announcement mic working.</p>", "s13-16"],
    ["bs 7909|temporary power event|distro tripped|rcd 30ma|generator earth", "<p>Event temporary power is designed and certified under <strong>BS 7909:2023+A1:2024</strong> by the power technician. Expect 30 mA RCD protection on sockets, inspection before switch-on, and earth rods on generator systems — don't disturb them. If the distro trips, tell the power tech; never reset someone else's breakers or bypass an RCD.</p>", "s13-17 s12-15"],
    /* d&b in more depth (matches §6.16–6.20) */
    ["what is a d&b setup|what is a setup on a d&b amp|d&b preset|which setup do i use", "<p>A <strong>setup</strong> is d&amp;b's word for a loudspeaker preset: the crossover, EQ and — most importantly — the protection for one exact box model. Each amp channel is told which box it's driving. Wrong setup means wrong sound at best and damaged drivers at worst, so it comes from the plan (ArrayCalc / R1 project), never a guess.</p>", "s6-16 s6-17"],
    ["what is dual channel mode|dual channel d&b|which socket for dual channel", "<p>In <strong>Dual Channel</strong> each amp channel drives its own box, and the cable goes in the channel's <strong>individual output</strong> (A, B, C or D). Both pin pairs carry that one channel, so any 2- or 4-core cable works.</p>", "s6-17"],
    ["what is mix top sub|mix top/sub|top and sub on one cable|centre output d&b", "<p><strong>Mix TOP/SUB</strong> sends a top and a sub down one 4-core cable from the <strong>centre A/B (or C/D)</strong> output: pins 1± carry the top (channel A or C), pins 2± the sub (B or D). The cable goes to the sub and links on to the top; each box only listens to its own pins. A 2-core cable here silences the sub.</p>", "s6-17 s3-12"],
    ["what is 2 way active|2-way active mode|active d&b box", "<p><strong>2-Way Active</strong> drives one box with two amp channels — one for the low end, one for the mid/high. It uses the centre A/B (or C/D) output: pins 1± LF, pins 2± MF/HF. Needs a 4-core cable and the right setups on both channels.</p>", "s6-17"],
    ["what is loadmatch|d&b loadmatch|cable length compensation", "<p><strong>LoadMatch</strong> lets current d&amp;b amps compensate electrically for the speaker cable, so a box at the end of a long run sounds like one on a short run. It needs the cable details entered (usually from the ArrayCalc/R1 project) — and it's the system tech's setting, not one to change on the day.</p>", "s6-16"],
    ["what is arrayprocessing|array processing d&b|should i change arrayprocessing", "<p><strong>ArrayProcessing</strong> is an ArrayCalc option that calculates individual filters for every box in a line array to shape its coverage and tone from front to back. It's applied through the amps. Never tweak it by hand — if it sounds wrong, tell the system tech.</p>", "s6-16"],
    ["what is cut mode|cut switch d&b|what does cut do|hfa|cpl|what is cpl|what is hfa", "<p>Switchable functions on a d&amp;b channel:</p><ul><li><strong>CUT</strong> — a high-pass that takes the low end out of a top when it's running with subs.</li><li><strong>HFA</strong> — gentle high-frequency roll-off for listeners close to the box.</li><li><strong>CPL</strong> — compensates for boxes coupling together at low frequencies in an array.</li></ul><p>Which ones are on is a design decision: copy them from the plan.</p>", "s6-17"],
    ["d20 vs d80|difference between d20 and d80|d40 vs d90|which d&b amp", "<p>The <strong>D20</strong> is the four-channel workhorse for E, Y, V, T, A and xS boxes (not J, M2 or SL). The <strong>D80</strong> is the high-power touring amp for larger systems. The newer <strong>D40</strong> and <strong>D90</strong> add Milan networking; the D90 drives the biggest systems. The tables in §6.20 say exactly which amp drives which box.</p>", "s6-16 s6-20"],
    ["d6 d12|old d&b amps|can bus r1|d12 pinout", "<p>The <strong>D6 and D12</strong> are older two-channel d&amp;b amps still in plenty of stock. They have a small LCD, connect to R1 over <strong>CAN bus</strong> (not Ethernet), and their output pinouts differ slightly from the current amps — read the label above the outputs every time.</p>", "s6-16 s6-17"],
    ["green light d&b|isp osp|what does green mean on the amp|signal present", "<p><strong>Green (ISP/OSP)</strong> means signal present: it's reaching the channel (brighter green on touchscreen amps = output signal present). Source playing but no green? The problem is upstream of the amp, or in its input routing.</p>", "s6-17"],
    ["red light d&b|ovl|amp shows red|channel red on d20|amp muted red", "<p>On D6/D12, <strong>red (OVL)</strong> is overload: input too hot or heavy limiting — turn down. On touchscreen amps the channel strip also shows red when a channel is <strong>muted</strong>; the screen says which. Find out who muted it and why before unmuting.</p>", "s6-17"],
    ["amp error message|over temperature|amp warning|amp keeps shutting down", "<p>Write down the <strong>exact</strong> error text from the amp screen or R1 and tell the system owner. Check the obvious — vents not blocked, rack not in direct sun, fans running — but don't keep power-cycling it; repeated restarts can hide the cause or make it worse.</p>", "s6-17 s12-13"],
    ["amp input mode|analogue or digital input d&b|fallback input|amp input routing", "<p>Each d&amp;b amp has an <strong>input mode</strong> (analogue, digital AES, or network) and each channel is routed from one or more inputs. Current amps can <strong>fall back</strong> to a second input if the first is lost — useful, but it's also why a channel can be playing from somewhere unexpected. Check routing against the plan.</p>", "s6-17"],
    ["d&b test generator|pink noise from amp|prove one box at a time|amp oscillator", "<p>Current d&amp;b amps have a built-in pink-noise and sine generator per channel. With mutes, it's the quickest way to prove one box at a time without anyone at the desk — agree it with the system tech first, start low, and warn people before sending noise.</p>", "s6-17 s11-9"],
    ["how many boxes per amp channel|boxes per channel|can i put 3 tops on one channel|parallel boxes d&b", "<p>Every d&amp;b box has a <strong>maximum number per amp channel</strong> — it depends on the box and the amp. The limits are in the §6.20 tables (and the d&amp;b manuals). Going over drops the load too low: the amp limits, sounds bad, or protects itself. Say “how many boxes per amp channel” and I'll walk you through it.</p>", "s6-20"],
    ["d&b nl8|nl8 output d20|amp nl8", "<p>The amp's <strong>NL8</strong> output carries all four channels on one 8-core multicore: pins 1± = A, 2± = B, 3± = C, 4± = D. A breakout at the other end splits it back to NL4s.</p>", "s6-17 s3-12"],
    ["d&b link socket|speaker link out|why do d&b boxes have two sockets", "<p>Each d&amp;b box has an input and a <strong>link</strong> socket wired straight through on all four poles. So one 4-core cable can feed a sub and link on to a top, and each box only listens to its own pins (tops pins 1, subs pins 2 on d&amp;b's usual convention). The pin use is printed beside every cabinet's connectors.</p>", "s6-17 s3-12"],
    ["ksl|gsl|sl series|cardioid line array|what is sl series", "<p>The <strong>SL-Series</strong> (KSL, GSL, XSL) are d&amp;b's large-format touring line arrays. They're cardioid by design — they control their own low end behind the array — and need the higher-power amps (D40, D80, D90, not D20). The §6.20 tables have the pairings.</p>", "s6-16 s6-20"],
    ["v series|y series|t series|e series|which d&b speaker is which|d&b ranges", "<p>Roughly: <strong>E and xS/A</strong> — point source, fills, small rooms; <strong>T</strong> — compact, switches between array and point source; <strong>Y and V</strong> — mid-to-large line arrays; <strong>J and SL</strong> — large touring arrays; <strong>M</strong> — wedges; <strong>-SUB and B</strong> — subs. Model-by-model in §6.20.</p>", "s6-16 s6-20"],
    ["d&b ds10|dante to d&b|how does dante get to a d20|ds10 bridge", "<p>D20 and D80 don't take Dante directly: it arrives through a bridge such as d&amp;b's <strong>DS10</strong>, which converts Dante to AES for the amps. D40/D90 have Milan built in instead. Find out how the feed reaches the amps during the advance.</p>", "s6-17 s8-10"],

    /* DM7 / desks more */
    ["dm7 sends on fader|how to do monitor mixes on dm7|sof", "<p><strong>Sends on Fader</strong> flips the faders to show every channel's send to one mix — the quickest way to build a monitor mix on a DM7. Choose the mix, then push the faders; turn it off to go back to the main mix. Keep monitor sends pre-fade.</p>", "s7-8 s7-9"],
    ["dm7 user defined keys|udk|assign a key dm7|talkback key dm7", "<p><strong>User Defined Keys</strong> are four banks of twelve touch keys on the DM7's 7\" screen. Set them up (Setup → User Setup) for what you need one press away: talkback, mute groups, a mix, a scene. Agree it with the engineer if it's their show file.</p>", "s7-8"],
    ["dm7 split mode|two desks in one|foh and monitors on one desk", "<p><strong>Split mode</strong> divides the full DM7 into two independent consoles in one frame — for example FOH and monitors, or two rooms. Each half has its own channels and mixes; check which half you're on before changing anything.</p>", "s7-8"],
    ["dm7 assist|dm7 ai|auto gain dm7|assist function", "<p><strong>Assist</strong> on the DM7 suggests head-amp gains, channel names and a starting fader balance from what it hears. Treat it as a starting point: check every gain on the meters and by ear, and ask the engineer before running it on their show.</p>", "s7-8"],
    ["dm7 firmware|update dm7|show file wont load dm7|dm7 version", "<p>Keep the DM7 on current firmware, and check a show file was made on the same or an older version before you load it — a file from newer firmware may not load or may behave differently. Yamaha publishes release notes on its DM7 support pages. Save the existing show file to USB first, always.</p>", "s7-8 s7-4"],
    ["dm7 editor|practise dm7 at home|offline dm7", "<p>The <strong>DM7 Editor</strong> is Yamaha's free offline software: the same screens as the desk on your laptop. Build and check show files before the job and practise patching, scenes and mixes. Yamaha's DM7 training videos are free too.</p>", "s7-8 s8-1"],
    ["shared stage box|gain compensation|foh and monitors share preamps", "<p>When FOH and monitors share the same stage-box preamps, one person's gain change moves everyone's levels. Yamaha desks offer <strong>gain compensation</strong> to keep the level sent to the network steady; even so, agree who owns the preamps and never change gain on a box you don't own.</p>", "s7-8 s7-10"],
    ["save show file|backup show file|how to name a show file|show file discipline", "<p>Before you touch a desk: save the current show file to USB with the <strong>date and job</strong> in the name. Save again after the line check and after the show. Never overwrite the only copy, and keep the engineer's file exactly as they gave it.</p>", "s7-4"],
    ["mute group|what is a mute group|everything muted desk", "<p>A <strong>mute group</strong> mutes several channels with one key — for example all the band mics between sets. If a whole section is silent, check whether a mute group is engaged before anything else.</p>", "s7-3 s7-7"],
    ["matrix|what is a matrix on a desk|matrix outputs", "<p>A <strong>matrix</strong> is a mixer after the main mixes: it combines buses (main L/R, subs, vocals…) into feeds for fills, delays, recording, broadcast or a lobby. On many systems the PA feeds come from matrices, so a matrix mute or level can silence one zone.</p>", "s7-3"],
    ["what is a hpf|high pass filter|low cut|where to set hpf", "<p>A <strong>high-pass filter</strong> (low cut) removes the low end below a set frequency. Use it on almost every channel that doesn't need bass — vocals around 80–120 Hz, speech higher. It cleans up the mix, reduces rumble and handling noise, and gives feedback less to work with.</p>", "s7-1 s16-1"],
    ["insert|what is an insert|insert point", "<p>An <strong>insert</strong> sends a channel's signal out to an external processor and back in, at a set point in the strip. On digital desks it's often a built-in plug-in. A bypassed or broken insert is a classic cause of a channel going silent or distorted.</p>", "s7-1 s16-10"],
    ["reverb send|how to add reverb|effects return", "<p>Add reverb with a <strong>post-fade aux send</strong> to the reverb unit, and bring the reverb back on its own return channel or stereo input. Start with a little — on vocals, a short plate or hall, high-passed so it doesn't muddy the low end.</p>", "s16-5"],
    ["de esser|sibilance|harsh s sounds|how to fix sibilance", "<p><strong>Sibilance</strong> (harsh s and t sounds, around 5–8 kHz) is tamed with a <strong>de-esser</strong> — a compressor that only reacts to that band. Also check the mic: a brighter mic, or a singer very close and on-axis, makes it worse.</p>", "s16-9"],
    ["why does the mix change with volume|fletcher munson|mix sounds different louder", "<p>Our ears hear bass and treble less at low volume, so a mix sounds thinner quiet and boomier loud. Mix at roughly the level the show will run, check it quiet and loud, and don't EQ in panic during a quiet soundcheck.</p>", "s16-7"],
    ["sidechain|ducking|what is a sidechain", "<p>A <strong>sidechain</strong> lets one signal control a processor on another — for example the voice ducking background music, or the kick triggering a compressor on the bass. On speech events, a ducker on playback keeps music under the presenter automatically.</p>", "s16-10"],

    /* connectors / power more */
    ["xlr cable test|how to test a cable|cable tester", "<p>Use a <strong>cable tester</strong>: plug both ends in and step through the pins — every pin should connect only to its partner. Wiggle the ends while testing to catch intermittent faults. Mark failures, quarantine them, and tell whoever repairs cables.</p>", "s3-11 s12-2"],
    ["adapter|xlr to jack|turnaround|gender changer|what adapters should i carry", "<p>Carry a small bag: XLR female-female and male-male turnarounds, XLR to TRS jack, mini-jack to twin jack or phono, phono to jack, a phase-reverse XLR (labelled!), and a couple of DIs. The adapter you don't have is the one you'll need.</p>", "s3-4 s19-17"],
    ["phone audio|connect a phone|play music from a phone|mini jack", "<p>Use a <strong>stereo DI</strong> (or a mini-jack to twin-jack cable into a DI) into two desk channels. Put the phone in airplane mode with Wi-Fi off so calls and notifications don't play through the PA, turn its volume most of the way up, and set gain at the desk.</p>", "s3-4 s3-9"],
    ["single phase three phase|what is three phase|is 3 phase dangerous", "<p><strong>Single phase</strong> is the ordinary 230 V supply. <strong>Three phase</strong> (400 V between phases) feeds bigger distribution, split across phases to balance the load. Anything three-phase, generators and distribution are for the qualified power tech — never open or reconfigure it.</p>", "s3-8 s9-6 s13-17"],
    ["how much power does an amp draw|amps per rack|power for audio|current draw", "<p>Big amps draw a lot at peaks. Use the manufacturer's figures (d&amp;b publishes power draw per amp and mode) and let the power tech size the supply. Don't daisy-chain amp racks off one extension, and spread racks across circuits as the plan says.</p>", "s3-8 s9-6"],
    ["audio and lighting power|dimmer noise|shared power with lighting", "<p>Keep audio power separate from lighting dimmers where you can, and run audio cables away from dimmer and LED power cables — dimmers in particular radiate buzz. If hum appears when lights change, that's the clue.</p>", "s9-2 s12-3"],
    ["rcd tripped|what is an rcd|rcd keeps tripping", "<p>An <strong>RCD</strong> trips when current leaks to earth — it's protecting someone from a shock. If it trips: don't keep resetting it. Unplug things one at a time to find the faulty item, quarantine it, and tell the power tech if it's their distribution.</p>", "s3-8 s12-15"],

    /* mics / RF more */
    ["lavalier|lav mic|lapel mic|how to hide a lav", "<p>A <strong>lavalier</strong> is a tiny clip-on mic. For speech: on the sternum, about a hand's width below the chin, on the side they turn to. For theatre, often at the hairline or over the ear for consistency. Tape the cable so it can't rub, and keep it off jewellery and clothing that rustles.</p>", "s10-11 s4-4"],
    ["headset mic|how to fit a headset mic|headset placement", "<p>Fit the headset so the capsule sits at the corner of the mouth, just out of the breath stream, and the boom doesn't touch the cheek. Ear hooks secure, cable down the back. Gain set with them speaking at full performance level.</p>", "s4-4 s10-11"],
    ["handheld radio mic|how to hold a mic|mic technique for presenters", "<p>Ask presenters to hold the mic by the body, about a fist away, pointed at the mouth — not at the chest — and never cupping the grille (it changes the sound and causes feedback). Brief them before they go on; it's the biggest single improvement to speech sound.</p>", "s4-3 s10-11"],
    ["wind noise|outdoor mic wind|windshield", "<p>Use a foam windshield (or a furry one outdoors), high-pass the channel, and keep mics out of the direct wind where possible. Omni and dynamic mics handle wind better than directional condensers.</p>", "s4-3 s6-14"],
    ["kick drum mic|drum mics|overhead mics|how many drum mics", "<p>A common kit: kick (inside and/or outside), snare top (sometimes bottom), hi-hat, toms, and a pair of overheads. Fewer mics can sound better — overheads plus kick and snare often do the job on small stages. Gate toms carefully; watch the 3:1 rule on overheads.</p>", "s4-5 s16-3"],
    ["acoustic guitar|how to mic an acoustic|piano mic", "<p>Acoustic guitar: a small condenser aimed where the neck meets the body, 15–30 cm away — or the guitar's pickup through a DI, often both. Piano: a pair of condensers inside over the strings, or a dedicated piano pickup on loud stages.</p>", "s4-5"],
    ["choir mic|how to mic a choir", "<p>A choir is best covered by a few condensers above and in front of the singers, about a metre away and high up, rather than one mic each. Keep them behind the PA, ring them out, and expect to rely on the room — choirs feed back easily if pushed.</p>", "s4-5"],
    ["transmitter gain|bodypack sensitivity|radio mic distorting", "<p>A radio mic distorting at the desk with a sensible desk gain usually means the <strong>transmitter</strong> sensitivity is too high for that voice. Turn it down on the pack (or in the receiver/coordination software), then reset the desk gain. Loud singers need less than quiet presenters.</p>", "s5-8 s5-11"],
    ["rf noise|hiss on radio mic|squelch|radio mic noise", "<p>Hiss or noise on a radio mic is usually weak RF — distance, body blocking, or antennas badly placed — or interference on that frequency. Check the receiver's RF meter while the performer moves; rescan if something new is on the frequency. Squelch mutes the receiver when the signal gets too weak to protect the PA.</p>", "s5-9 s12-11"],
    ["iem frequency|iem licence|in ears need licence", "<p>IEM transmitters are radio transmitters too: they need to be inside your licence (Channel 38, Channel 70 or a site licence) and coordinated with the radio mics to avoid intermodulation. Keep IEM transmitters and their antennas away from the mic receivers' antennas.</p>", "s5-7 s5-12"],
    ["shure axient|axient digital|showlink", "<p><strong>Axient Digital</strong> is Shure's top-tier digital radio system, controlled and monitored over the network from Wireless Workbench. ShowLink lets transmitter settings change remotely; frequency diversity options protect against drop-outs. Set it up with the system owner's coordination.</p>", "s5-11 s5-2"],
    ["sennheiser ew-dx|ewdx|sennheiser control cockpit|wsm", "<p><strong>EW-DX</strong> is Sennheiser's digital radio range. Coordinate with Sennheiser's Wireless Systems Manager or Control Cockpit (or the receiver's scan for a small system), sync each pack, set gain, label and walk test — the same shape as any system.</p>", "s5-11"],

    /* monitors */
    ["personal monitor mixer|aviom|personal mixers|my mix", "<p><strong>Personal mixers</strong> let each musician set their own monitor mix from a set of feeds you send them. Send clearly labelled, sensibly grouped stems (vocals, keys, drums…), check every feed at line check, and keep a master volume they can find.</p>", "s15-5"],
    ["ambient mic|crowd mic|audience mic iem", "<p>An <strong>ambient (audience) mic</strong> feeds the room sound into IEM mixes so performers don't feel cut off. Place a pair at the stage edge facing the crowd, and keep them out of the PA mix.</p>", "s15-4"],
    ["side fills|drum fill|monitor speakers on stage", "<p><strong>Side fills</strong> are speakers at the sides of the stage giving everyone a general mix; a <strong>drum fill</strong> is a dedicated monitor for the drummer, often with a sub. Ring them out like wedges and keep the level only as high as needed.</p>", "s15-1 s15-2"],
    ["who mixes monitors|monitor engineer job|no monitor engineer", "<p>On bigger shows a <strong>monitor engineer</strong> mixes the performers' wedges and IEMs from a separate desk side-stage. On smaller ones FOH does both. Either way, the performer's comfort comes first — a happy band plays better.</p>", "s15-9 s15-1"],
    ["custom iems|custom moulds|universal earpieces", "<p><strong>Custom</strong> moulds fit the performer's ears exactly — better isolation and comfort, but personal. <strong>Universal</strong> earpieces use foam or silicone tips and work for anyone. Never share someone else's customs; clean universal tips between users.</p>", "s15-12"],
    /* networks more */
    ["what is aes67|aes67 vs dante|dante aes67 mode", "<p><strong>AES67</strong> isn't a competing product but a common language that lets Dante and other systems exchange audio directly, without a converter. It needs careful clock and multicast setup — use it only where the design calls for it.</p>", "s8-2 s3-7"],
    ["dante latency|what latency for dante|dante latency setting", "<p>Dante latency is set per receiving device (commonly 1 ms on a small network, more for more switch hops). Too low for the network and you get clicks or drop-outs; the latency warnings in Dante Controller tell you. Match the system design.</p>", "s8-2 s8-9"],
    ["dante device missing|cant see dante device|dante controller empty", "<p>Check in order: cable and switch port lights, laptop on the audio network by cable with Wi-Fi off, the right network interface chosen in Dante Controller, IP addressing (DHCP vs 169.254 link-local), and that the device isn't on the secondary network only.</p>", "s8-9 s12-14"],
    ["dante clock leader wrong|two leaders|clock unlocked dante", "<p>Exactly <strong>one</strong> device should be Dante leader — normally the one the design names (often the desk). Set Preferred Leader on that device only. Two leaders, or a device on a different sample rate, gives clicks, drop-outs or no audio.</p>", "s8-9 s2-9"],
    ["dante virtual soundcard|dvs|record over dante", "<p><strong>Dante Virtual Soundcard</strong> turns a computer into a Dante device for multitrack recording and playback (up to 64 channels at 48 kHz). Install it with Dante Controller, set the latency for the network, route channels in Dante Controller, then pick it as the DAW's audio device.</p>", "s7-9 s14-7"],
    ["fibre|optical cable|fiber snake|why fibre", "<p><strong>Fibre</strong> carries network or digital audio much further than copper (hundreds of metres to kilometres) with no ground loops or interference. Keep the ends capped and clean — dirt on a fibre connector is a classic fault — and never bend it tightly.</p>", "s3-6 s3-7"],
    ["wifi for audio|control desk on ipad|ipad mixing wifi", "<p>Wi-Fi is fine for <strong>remote control</strong> (an iPad mixing app, R1 walk-around) but never for audio itself. Use a dedicated router on the control network, not the venue's guest Wi-Fi, and keep a wired fallback.</p>", "s8-6"],

    /* test and site */
    ["soundcheck order|what order soundcheck|running a soundcheck", "<p>Line check first (every input works), then the band: drums, bass, guitars, keys, vocals — each on its own, then together. Monitors get sorted alongside. Finish with a song or two at show level, then save the scene.</p>", "s11-1 s16-6"],
    ["what to do first on site|arriving at a venue|first thing at a gig", "<p>Find the person in charge and get the induction, then: where the power is, where the PA and desk go, access routes and parking, fire exits. Unload safely, then build in the plan's order. Ask early — the first hour sets up the whole day.</p>", "s1-4 s19-4"],
    ["what to wear on site|blacks|dress code crew", "<p>Usually <strong>blacks</strong> (plain black clothing) for anything the audience might see, safety boots for load-in and out, and layers — venues are cold at 7 am and hot under lights. Corporate jobs may want smarter blacks.</p>", "s1-6 s13-11"],
    ["running late|late for a job|delayed at load in", "<p>Tell your lead as soon as you know, with an honest time. On site, if the schedule slips, say early what it means for the line check or soundcheck rather than quietly rushing — rushed checks are where faults get missed.</p>", "s1-6 s1-4"],
    ["changeover|festival changeover|fast changeover", "<p>Prep everything you can off-stage (mics on stands, cables looped, risers wheeled), have the next act's patch and stage plot printed, and work in a fixed order. Line check as soon as each input is plugged. Calm and in order is faster than rushing.</p>", "s10-10"],
    ["patch list|input list|how to read an input list", "<p>An <strong>input list</strong> numbers every source (kick in, snare top, vocal 1…) with the mic, stand and stage-box input. Patch to that list exactly and label the stage box; when things change, update the list so everyone's on the same page.</p>", "s1-5 s11-2"],
    ["stage plot|what is a stage plot", "<p>A <strong>stage plot</strong> is a drawing of where everyone and everything goes on stage — performers, risers, wedges, DIs, power. Use it to place stage boxes and run cables before the band arrives.</p>", "s1-5 s1-7"],
    ["label cables|how to label|labelling", "<p>Label <strong>both ends</strong> of every cable and every stage-box input with the same thing (channel number and source). Coloured tape for zones or systems helps. Good labels make the derig and every fault-find faster.</p>", "s3-10 s11-2"],
    ["what goes in a fault kit|spares to take|spare kit", "<p>A fault kit: spare XLRs, a couple of NL4s, DIs, a spare SM58 and radio mic, batteries, adapters, a cable tester, gaffa and electrical tape, a torch, a small multimeter, and the manuals or this app. Spares already tested, not ‘probably fine’.</p>", "s12-2 s19-17"],

    /* safety more */
    ["is it safe to touch a speaker cable|is speaker cable dangerous|speaker cable shock|speaker cable live|can speaker level shock you", "<p>Speaker-level outputs on big amps carry enough voltage to give a painful or dangerous shock. <strong>Mute the amp channel</strong> before plugging or unplugging speaker cables, never touch exposed conductors, and never re-terminate a cable on a live system.</p>", "s3-12 s13-2"],
    ["flown speakers|rigging safety|walking under a hang|line array rigging", "<p>Don't stand or work under a load being flown, and don't touch rigging, motors or truss unless you're trained and authorised. The rigger and system tech own the hang; your job is to keep the area clear and report anything that looks wrong.</p>", "s6-5 s13-3"],
    ["tower|ladder|can i use a ladder|scaffold tower", "<p>Use ladders only for short, light work with three points of contact and someone footing it where needed. Towers need PASMA-trained people to build and move them. Never work at height alone, and never on a stage edge without protection.</p>", "s13-3"],
    ["tired|long hours|fatigue|exhausted at work", "<p>Fatigue causes most mistakes and many accidents. Take your breaks, eat, drink water, and say if you're too tired to drive home safely — the company should plan for it. A long build day plus a late show is exactly when to slow down and double-check.</p>", "s13-9 s13-15"],
    ["first aid|someone injured|accident on site", "<p>Make the area safe, get the site first aider (know who it is from the induction), and call 999 for anything serious. Don't move an injured person unless they're in danger. Report it afterwards so it's recorded.</p>", "s13-10 s13-13"],
    ["pyro|confetti|haze|special effects safety", "<p>Special effects belong to licensed specialists. Know when they fire (the cue sheet), keep out of exclusion zones, protect mics and desk from confetti and haze residue, and tell people with asthma before haze goes up.</p>", "s9-12 s13-14"],
    ["electric shock|someone got a shock", "<p>Don't touch them if they may still be in contact with the supply — isolate the power at the source first. Then get first aid and call 999. Quarantine the equipment involved and report it; it's RIDDOR-reportable if serious.</p>", "s13-2 s13-13"],

    /* events, video, lighting */
    ["led wall interference|led screen noise|video wall rf", "<p>LED walls and their processors can radiate RF noise and buzz. Keep radio mic antennas and receivers away from them, scan with the wall <strong>on</strong>, and route audio cables away from the wall's power and data.</p>", "s9-15 s5-9"],
    ["video audio sync|lip sync|audio ahead of video", "<p>Video processing (LED walls, cameras, switchers) delays the picture, so live audio arrives first — and lip sync looks wrong on screens. The fix is usually delay on the audio <strong>to the screens or stream</strong>, agreed with the video team; never delay the PA to match a screen.</p>", "s9-13 s9-14"],
    ["hdmi audio|audio from a laptop to screen|embedded audio", "<p>Laptops often send audio down HDMI to the projector or switcher instead of the PA. Choose the right output on the laptop, or take audio from the video switcher's audio output, a USB interface or a DI on the headphone out — agreed with the video team.</p>", "s9-13 s8-4"],
    ["dmx|what is dmx|lighting control", "<p><strong>DMX</strong> is the control protocol lighting uses: up to 512 channels per universe, daisy-chained on 5-pin (sometimes 3-pin) cable. It looks like XLR but isn't audio — never mix DMX and mic cables, and don't use one in place of the other.</p>", "s9-7 s12-16"],
    ["working with lighting|lighting crew|truss space", "<p>Agree space and routes early: truss, cable routes, power and FOH position. Keep audio cables away from dimmer feeds, and tell lighting if their kit causes buzz. Shared good manners save everyone time.</p>", "s9-2 s9-5"],
    ["awards ceremony|gala dinner audio|awards show", "<p>Awards jobs are corporate jobs with a script: walk-up music, a lectern and host radio mics, winners' speeches, video stings. Rehearse the running order with the stage manager, keep spare handhelds hot, and have the next cue ready before the last one finishes.</p>", "s10-2 s10-11"],
    ["church|house of worship|place of worship audio", "<p>Places of worship mix speech, music and often a permanent install you don't own. Keep speech clear, feedback away from lapel mics, check the hearing loop, and don't change the install's settings without the venue's say-so.</p>", "s10-6 s6-11"],
    ["theatre radio mic|mic a cast|theatre mic placement", "<p>Theatre mics usually sit at the hairline or over the ear, taped and colour-matched, with packs in pouches. Change batteries on a schedule, check every mic before each show, and keep a written plot of who's on which channel.</p>", "s10-4 s19-19"],

    /* recording / broadcast */
    ["broadcast loudness|lufs|ebu r128|stream level", "<p>Broadcast and streaming platforms work to a <strong>loudness target</strong> measured in LUFS (EBU R128 is −23 LUFS for UK/EU broadcast; streams often aim louder). Use a loudness meter on the feed, not just peak meters, and agree the target with the broadcaster.</p>", "s18-17 s14-5"],
    ["backup recording|second recorder|redundant recording", "<p>If a recording matters, run a <strong>backup</strong>: a second computer, a stereo recorder off the matrix, or the desk's own USB recording. Start both, check both are moving, and keep them on different power if you can.</p>", "s14-12"],
    ["hand over files|deliver recordings|naming recording files", "<p>Before handing over: check every file plays, name files and folders clearly (date, event, track names), include a track list, and copy to the client's drive plus a backup. Don't delete your copy until they confirm they have theirs.</p>", "s14-10 s14-13"],
    ["zoom room|teams room|video conference audio|remote presenter", "<p>For a remote presenter: send the call a mix-minus (everything except the call's own return), bring the call back into a desk channel, and test both directions with a real call before doors. Mute the room mics into the call during playback.</p>", "s10-8"],

    /* acoustics / measurement */
    ["room acoustics|echoey room|reverberant room|why does the room sound bad", "<p>Hard, empty rooms are reverberant: speech blurs and bass booms. Aim the PA at the audience (soft) and away from walls and ceilings (hard), keep levels sensible, use more, closer speakers rather than one loud one, and expect it to improve when people come in.</p>", "s6-15"],
    ["room mode|standing wave|bass different in places", "<p>A <strong>room mode</strong> is a low-frequency resonance set by the room's dimensions — bass is strong in some spots and missing in others with nothing wrong with the system. Moving subs or the listening position changes it; EQ only helps a little.</p>", "s6-15"],
    ["measurement mic|which measurement mic|calibrated mic", "<p>A <strong>measurement mic</strong> is an omni condenser with a flat response (often with a calibration file). It's used for tuning and SPL readings, not for shows. Treat it gently and keep it in its case.</p>", "s6-8 s8-11"],
    ["sound level meter|how to measure spl|db meter app", "<p>Use a proper sound level meter (or the noise-management system the venue uses) set to the weighting and time the limit is written in — usually dB(A) averaged over a set period, sometimes dB(C) for bass. Phone apps are only a rough guide.</p>", "s6-10 s8-3"],
    ["delay speaker|fill speaker|front fill|out fill", "<p><strong>Fills</strong> cover areas the main PA misses: front fills for the first rows, out-fills at the sides, delays further back. Each is time-aligned to the main PA (so it sounds like one system) and set quieter, so the main still feels like the source.</p>", "s6-6 s6-8"],
    ["splay angle|line array angles|why are the boxes angled", "<p>The <strong>splay angles</strong> between line-array boxes shape where the sound goes: tighter at the top (throwing far), wider at the bottom (covering close). They come from the prediction software (ArrayCalc, Soundvision) and are set by the rigger and system tech, not adjusted by ear.</p>", "s6-6 s6-7"],

    /* career / learning more */
    ["how to get more responsibility|step up at work|ask for more", "<p>Do the basics brilliantly, then ask specifically: “Could I run the line check on the next job?” or “Can I shadow the system tech's tuning?” Show you know where your limits are, and log what you've done so the next step is easy to justify.</p>", "s17-6 s17-7"],
    ["portfolio|evidence of work|cv for sound", "<p>Keep a simple log of every job: date, venue, kit, your role, what you learned, and photos (with permission). It becomes your CV, your evidence for training, and your answer to “what have you done?”.</p>", "s17-7"],
    ["networking in the industry|how to meet people|get more work", "<p>Be the person people want back: early, calm, helpful, honest about what you don't know. Stay in touch with engineers and companies you've worked with, join a trade body or community, and say yes to the odd unglamorous job — that's where work comes from.</p>", "s17-3 s18-9"],
    ["what should i learn next|next skill|what to practise", "<p>Pick one thing from the next stage of the Pathway and practise it on your next real job — a line check on your own, a monitor mix, a Dante patch, a system walk test. The Pathway screen has the order; say “what should I learn next” for a suggestion.</p>", "s17-1 s17-4"],
    ["free courses|free training audio|learn for free", "<p>Plenty is free: Dante certification (Audinate), manufacturer academies (d&amp;b, Yamaha, Shure, Sennheiser), offline desk editors, and well-known YouTube channels. Part 18 lists the good ones by topic.</p>", "s18-1 s18-14"],
    ["union|bectu|pay rates|am i being paid fairly", "<p><strong>BECTU</strong> (part of Prospect) is the UK union for entertainment crew, with guidance on rates, contracts and hours. It's worth knowing the going rates and your rights under the Working Time Regulations.</p>", "s17-2 s18-12"],
    ["touring abroad|working in europe|carnet|touring outside uk", "<p>Touring outside the UK means paperwork: the right to work in each country, a <strong>carnet</strong> (or similar) for the equipment, insurance, and sometimes different power and radio frequency rules. The production manager handles most of it — know what applies to you.</p>", "s17-8"],
    ["what is r1|how to use r1|r1 step by step|connect r1 to amps|r1 go online", "<p>Open the system tech's R1 project, join the amp control network by cable (Wi-Fi off, laptop on the amps' IP range), connect, and check every channel against the plan. When R1 asks which way to synchronise, <strong>don't send the project to the amps unless you're sure</strong> — that overwrites them. Ask the system tech.</p>", "s6-22 s6-16"],
    ["r1 wink|find an amp in r1|which amp is which", "<p>Use R1's <strong>Wink</strong> function: it flashes that amp's display so you can find it in the rack. Each amp needs a unique Remote ID — duplicates cause errors.</p>", "s6-22"],
    ["r1 system check|d&b system check|check every box r1", "<p><strong>System check</strong> measures each box against a stored reference to catch a dead driver or a wrong cable. The system tech sets the reference; you can run the check and report what it flags.</p>", "s6-22 s11-9"],
    ["update amp firmware|should i update the amps|firmware show day", "<p>Not on a show day. Firmware updates can change behaviour and occasionally fail part-way — leave them to the system owner, planned in the warehouse, not on site.</p>", "s6-22"],
    ["how to set up comms|clearcom setup|intercom setup|partyline", "<p>Get the channel plan from whoever owns comms, power the main station, run the lines and daisy-chain the packs (wired), or charge and register the packs (wireless). Then check every pack: talk, listen and call light on each channel. Label comms cable so it isn't borrowed for a mic.</p>", "s8-12 s11-8"],
    ["laptop playback setup|reliable playback|stop laptop notifications|playback checklist", "<p>Mains in; Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, notifications, sleep and updates off; out through a proper interface or DI; files local and in order; levels set at the desk; a backup source ready on another channel; every cue rehearsed.</p>", "s8-13 s8-4"],
    ["do i get sick pay|statutory sick pay|ssp|sick pay freelance|off sick", "<p>Since 6 April 2026 <strong>Statutory Sick Pay</strong> is paid from the first day off sick, with no minimum earnings to qualify: £123.25 a week (2026/27) or 80% of average weekly earnings, whichever is lower. It's for employees — genuinely self-employed freelancers don't get it.</p>", "s13-15"],
    ["holiday pay|how much holiday|holiday for casual work|rolled up holiday pay|12.07", "<p>Everyone gets 5.6 weeks' paid holiday a year, pro rata. Casual and irregular-hours crew build it up at <strong>12.07% of hours worked</strong>, or the employer pays it \"rolled up\" as an extra 12.07% shown separately on each payslip.</p>", "s13-15"],
    ["zero hours contract|zero hours rights|guaranteed hours|shift cancelled pay", "<p>From <strong>April 2027</strong> zero- and low-hours workers get the right to be offered guaranteed hours matching what they regularly work, reasonable notice of shifts, and compensation for late-cancelled or shortened shifts (Employment Rights Act 2025). Details are still being finalised — check ACAS.</p>", "s13-15"],
    ["who pays for ppe|do i have to buy my own boots|free ppe|ppe for casual workers", "<p>If you're a <strong>worker</strong> (casual crew, not genuinely self-employed), PPE the job requires must be provided free — that's been the law since April 2022. Genuinely self-employed freelancers provide their own.</p>", "s13-11"],
    ["peak noise limit|137 db|140 db peak|impulse noise", "<p>Besides the daily averages (80/85/87 dB(A)), the Noise at Work Regulations set <strong>peak</strong> values for sudden bangs: 135 dB(C) lower action, 137 dB(C) upper action, 140 dB(C) limit.</p>", "s13-5"],
    ["opt out notice|cancel the 48 hour opt out|withdraw opt out", "<p>You can cancel a 48-hour opt-out with 7 days' notice, or longer if the opt-out agreement says so — never more than 3 months. It must have been voluntary and in writing in the first place.</p>", "s13-15"],
    /* coverage of the rest of the book */
    ["what do the markers mean|standard also seen watch it go deeper|what does standard mean in the book", "<p>Everything in the book is tagged: <strong>Standard</strong> — the normal way, your default; <strong>Also seen</strong> — a common alternative, not wrong; <strong>Watch it</strong> — where things go wrong or get dangerous; <strong>Go deeper</strong> — where to learn more.</p>", "s0-1"],
    ["how do i use this app|how does treebysound work|where do i find things|navigating the app", "<p><strong>Ask</strong> answers questions and walks you through faults and set-ups. <strong>Job</strong> is for an actual booking (checklists, system test, workspace). <strong>Support</strong> is for the moment something's wrong (make it safe, calculators, what to say). <strong>Pathway</strong> is your learning route. <strong>Library</strong> is the whole handbook, searchable.</p>", "s0-2"],
    ["lifecycle of a hire|how does a hire work|what happens to kit between jobs", "<p>Every job loops: enquiry and kit list → prep (pull, test, pack) → load out → load in and build → test → show → derig → return, check-in and repair. Knowing where you are on the loop tells you what your job is right now.</p>", "s1-2"],
    ["signal chain|signal flow|how does sound get from the mic to the speaker", "<p>Every system is the same chain: <strong>source</strong> → <strong>transducer</strong> (mic or DI) → <strong>preamp</strong> → <strong>processing and mixing</strong> → <strong>amplifier</strong> → <strong>loudspeaker</strong> → ears. Fault-finding is just following that chain until the signal stops.</p>", "s2-1 s12-1"],
    ["mic stands|which mic stand|tall boom short boom|round base or tripod", "<p><strong>Tall booms</strong> for vocals and overheads, <strong>short booms</strong> for snare, toms, kick and guitar cabs (pack more than you think), <strong>straight stands</strong> for speech, <strong>desk stands</strong> for panels. Round bases are tidier and safer on stage; tripods are lighter but trip people.</p>", "s4-7"],
    ["how does a speaker work|what is a horn|passive vs active speaker|what is a crossover", "<p>A speaker has <strong>drivers</strong> (cones for lows, a compression driver on a horn for highs) and a <strong>crossover</strong> that splits the frequencies between them. <strong>Passive</strong> boxes have the crossover inside and need an external amp; <strong>active</strong> boxes have amps and processing built in; d&amp;b-style systems put the processing in the amp instead.</p>", "s6-1 s6-4"],
    ["speaker brands|which brands will i meet|pa brands", "<p>On UK hire and touring work: d&amp;b audiotechnik, L-Acoustics, Martin Audio, Meyer Sound, and on smaller jobs RCF, QSC, Electro-Voice and others. The ideas are the same everywhere — amps, presets/setups, prediction software, remote control — with different names.</p>", "s6-3 s6-21"],
    ["interpretation system|simultaneous interpretation|translation booth|ir receivers", "<p>Interpreters in booths listen on headphones and speak the translation into a mic, which goes out on IR or RF to delegates' receivers. Your part is usually the feeds: a clean floor feed to the booths, the booth outputs to the transmitters, and checking every channel and receiver before doors.</p>", "s6-12"],
    ["audio description|describer|blind audience headsets", "<p><strong>Audio description</strong> narrates the visual action between dialogue for blind and partially-sighted audience members on headsets. Specialist describers run it; your job is making sure the infrastructure works — the describer's mic, the transmitter and every headset — and checking it before each show.</p>", "s6-13"],
    ["consoles i will meet|which mixing desks|common desks", "<p>Most often: Yamaha (DM7, CL/QL, TF, RIVAGE), Allen &amp; Heath (SQ, dLive, Avantis), DiGiCo (SD and Quantum), Midas/Behringer (M32, X32, Wing, HD96). Learn the one your company uses on its free offline editor, then the channel strip carries over to the rest.</p>", "s7-2 s7-10"],
    ["rental management software|rentman|current rms|hirehop|what is an rms", "<p>Hire companies run on <strong>rental management software</strong> — Rentman, Current RMS, HireHop and others — for kit lists, availability, crew and quotes. Scanning kit in and out properly and flagging faults in it is what keeps stock accurate and makes you visible to the people who book crew.</p>", "s8-7"],
    ["backline|band's own amps|working with backline techs|stage volume", "<p><strong>Backline</strong> is the instruments themselves — amps, drums, keys. If it's yours to mic, see §4.5; if the band has a backline tech, your role is patching their DIs and mics and agreeing stage volume with them — loud backline is the enemy of a clean FOH mix.</p>", "s9-4 s4-5"],
    ["avolites titan|what is titan|avokey|titan pc suite|lighting software", "<p><strong>Titan</strong> is Avolites' lighting control software: it runs their consoles (Tiger Touch, Arena, Sapphire, Quartz) and, with an <strong>AvoKey</strong> licence, on a PC as Titan PC Suite and the Simulator. Download it from avolites.com, plug the AvoKey in, and simulate the desk your company uses.</p>", "s9-8 s9-9"],
    ["titan basics|how to start with titan|patch fixtures titan|titan concepts", "<p>Start in the Simulator: patch a few fixtures (choose the right personality and mode), make palettes for colour and position, build a cue list, and play it back. Those concepts — patch, palettes, cues, playbacks — carry across every lighting desk.</p>", "s9-10 s9-9"],
    ["why learn lighting|lighting as a second skill|does titan help audio", "<p>Lighting skills make you more useful on small jobs where one person covers both, turn DMX faults from a mystery into a checklist, and are a genuine second specialism. It's a real, marketable skill on top of audio.</p>", "s9-11 s21-6"],
    ["streaming tools|obs|streaming software|vmix", "<p>Common streaming and recording tools: <strong>OBS</strong> (free), <strong>vMix</strong> and hardware like the Blackmagic ATEM switchers. Your audio job is a clean, separate stream mix at the right loudness, delivered to whatever the video team uses.</p>", "s9-16 s14-6"],
    ["why does context matter|different types of event|corporate vs gig", "<p>The same kit does different jobs: a gig wants impact and energy, corporate wants every word clear and invisible sound, theatre wants exact repeatability, broadcast wants a clean feed. Work out which world you're in on arrival — it changes what “good” means.</p>", "s10-1 s10-7"],
    ["live music touring|band show|club gig|what is touring like", "<p>Band and club shows are fast and loud: full band inputs, monitors that matter as much as FOH, quick changeovers and a mix that serves the energy of the music. Touring adds the same show night after night in different rooms — routine and labelling are everything.</p>", "s10-3"],
    ["broadcast work|outside broadcast|tv sound|a2 job", "<p>Broadcast sound runs from A2 (rigging radio mics and comms on presenters and contributors) up to sound supervisor. It's precise, deadline-driven and comms-heavy, with loudness and feeds held to a standard. §21.2 covers it as a career.</p>", "s10-5 s21-2"],
    ["reading the room|how formal should i be|what kind of job is this", "<p>Read the signals on arrival: a running order timed to the minute means formal, cued discipline; “we'll sort set times later” means stay flexible and quick. Match your energy and formality to the job — not a different person, just the right register.</p>", "s10-7"],
    ["signing off a line check|when is line check done|line check complete", "<p>“Done” means every input individually proved — not a song played that sounded fine. Tell whoever's running the show explicitly that line check is complete, and name anything still outstanding: “all good except channel 22, waiting on a replacement cable”.</p>", "s11-7"],
    ["common faults quick reference|fault patterns|most likely cause", "<p>The quick patterns: total silence on one channel → mute, fader, gain, patch, cable; crackle when touched → a connector; steady hum → ground loop; whine that follows a dimmer or screen → interference; distorted at low fader → input gain too hot. §12.6 has the full table.</p>", "s12-6"],
    ["mic faults|cable faults|mic sounds wrong|bad mic", "<p>Work from the source: mic switched on, phantom if it's a condenser, swap the cable for a known-good one, then a different stage-box input. The fault follows whatever you just swapped. A mic that sounds dull or thin may be damaged — try a spare of the same model.</p>", "s12-9 s3-11"],
    ["podcast recording|small recording|record an interview|simple recording", "<p>Small jobs don't need the full multitrack setup: a small interface (a Focusrite Scarlett or similar) into a laptop, one mic per voice, headphones to check, levels peaking well below the top, and a test recording played back before you start.</p>", "s14-9"],
    ["one ear silent iem|iem one side|monitor world faults", "<p>One ear silent on IEMs: a cable or connector fault at the belt pack, or a panned mix with nothing on that side. “I can't hear myself” mid-song: usually a level request, sometimes a channel actually dropped — check the meter, not just the fader.</p>", "s15-6 s15-3"],
    ["build a wedge mix|wedge mix start to finish|monitor mix wedge", "<p>Start with the performer's own source at a comfortable level, add only what they ask for, ring the wedge out first, keep the sends pre-fade, and turn down what masks them before turning them up. Check in between songs — it's a conversation.</p>", "s15-11 s15-8"],
    ["delay effect|slapback|tempo delay|echo effect", "<p>A <strong>delay</strong> repeats the signal as distinct echoes: a <strong>slapback</strong> (one short repeat) thickens a vocal; a <strong>tempo-synced</strong> delay (quarter or dotted-eighth) is an obvious effect. Route it like reverb: a post-fade aux send to a shared return.</p>", "s16-8"],
    ["conservation|bioacoustics|ecology work|conservation career|wildlife sound", "<p>Part 18.15 is for keeping a conservation and field-survey career active alongside audio — and building <strong>bioacoustics</strong> (recording and analysing wildlife sound), where audio skills are a genuine advantage. It points to the bodies, tools and recording schemes worth knowing.</p>", "s18-15"],
    ["essential reading|books about live sound|what should i read", "<p>Part 18.7 lists the books and references worth your time, and §18.16 the trade press (Sound On Sound and others). Read a little often — one article or chapter a week adds up fast.</p>", "s18-7 s18-16"],
    ["theatre show control|qlab cues|show control|cueing", "<p>Theatre runs on cues: QLab (or similar) fires sound cues, often triggered by the stage manager's calls or by show control messages from other departments. §18.13 lists where to learn it properly.</p>", "s18-13 s8-4"],
    ["other careers|where can audio take me|career options|change direction", "<p>Live audio leads to broadcast and OB, AV integration and install, studio and post-production, game audio and esports, lighting and video, production and technical management, venue and festival management, training and technical sales, and acoustics consultancy. Part 21 covers each.</p>", "s21-1 s21-12"],
    ["av installation career|systems integration job|install work", "<p>AV integration means installing and commissioning fixed systems — speakers, DSPs, control and video — and programming what one “Start meeting” button actually does. Your live audio background helps with DSP, signal flow and fault-finding; programming (Q-SYS, Crestron and similar) is the skill to add.</p>", "s21-3"],
    ["studio work|post production career|music technology", "<p>Studio and post-production are quieter, deadline-driven work on recording, editing and mixing. Live skills transfer (gain, EQ, signal flow); the extra is DAW speed and critical listening. §21.4 covers the routes in.</p>", "s21-4"],
    ["esports|game audio career|gaming events", "<p><strong>Esports production</strong> is a live-events job with a gaming audience — arena shows, caster and player comms, broadcast feeds — and it's growing fast in the UK. <strong>Game audio</strong> is studio sound design and implementation for games. §21.5 covers both.</p>", "s21-5"],
    ["production manager career|technical manager|move into management", "<p>Production and technical management is planning, budgets, people and safety rather than hands-on kit — it suits organised people who like seeing the whole show. Start by advancing jobs well and running small crews.</p>", "s21-7 s21-8"],
    ["acoustics career|institute of acoustics|noise consultant", "<p>Acoustics consultancy (room design, noise assessment) usually needs further study; the Institute of Acoustics' Diploma in Acoustics and Noise Control is a part-time route alongside work. Compliance work (noise management at events) is a closer step from live sound.</p>", "s21-10"],
    ["try a new direction|test a career change|explore without quitting", "<p>Pick one direction and be specific, tell the right people you're curious, take one small job or shadow day in it, and log what you learned. None of it needs quitting anything first.</p>", "s21-11"],
    ["sales job audio|technical sales|warehouse manager|training role", "<p>Technical sales, training and warehouse or operations leadership all value people who know kit from the field. They suit people who explain things well or like running systems and teams. §21.9 covers them.</p>", "s21-9"],
    ["regulations list|which laws apply|safety law overview", "<p>The main ones: Electricity at Work 1989, PUWER 1998, LOLER 1998, Manual Handling 1992, Noise at Work 2005, CDM 2015, Work at Height 2005, RIDDOR 2013, COSHH 2002 and the Working Time Regulations 1998 — plus Martyn's Law from 2027. §13.1 has what each means for you.</p>", "s13-1 s13-16"],
    ["martin audio|wavefront precision|wpc|ikon amp|display 3|vu-net", "<p><strong>Martin Audio</strong> works like d&amp;b: separate <strong>iKON</strong> amps in racks, Wavefront Precision line arrays (WPC, WPS, WPM, WPL), <strong>DISPLAY</strong> to design and optimise the array (per-box DSP included) and <strong>VU-NET</strong> to control and monitor it. The optimisation is the system tech's design — don't tweak it.</p>", "s6-23"],
    ["meyer sound|self powered speakers|panther|leopard|galaxy processor|nebra|mapp", "<p><strong>Meyer Sound</strong> boxes are <strong>self-powered</strong>: amp and processing inside every box, so each needs mains <em>and</em> signal. Arrays are designed in <strong>MAPP 3D</strong>, processed by <strong>GALAXY</strong>, and monitored in <strong>Nebra</strong>. Power distribution up the stack is part of the system — plan it with the power and system techs.</p>", "s6-23"],
    ["self powered vs passive|active vs passive pa|why does each speaker need power", "<p><strong>Passive</strong> systems (d&amp;b, L-Acoustics, Martin Audio) run speaker cables from amp racks. <strong>Self-powered</strong> systems (Meyer and many smaller active boxes) put the amp in each box, so every box needs mains and a signal — fewer racks, more power distribution.</p>", "s6-23 s6-1"],
    ["digico|digico session|digico snapshot|recall scope|digico quick start|sd12", "<p>On a DiGiCo, a <strong>session</strong> is the whole show (“one band”) and a <strong>snapshot</strong> changes values within it (“one song”). <strong>Recall scope</strong> decides what each snapshot changes — head-amp gains, for example, may not be included. Save the session first, keep snapshots simple, and learn it on the free offline software.</p>", "s7-11"],
    ["dlive|avantis|allen and heath|mixrack|recall filter|dlive director", "<p><strong>dLive</strong> keeps the processing and I/O in the <strong>MixRack</strong>, with the Surface as the control desk; <strong>Avantis</strong> is all-in-one. Sockets are named R-nn (MixRack), S-nn (Surface), DX, P (I/O card). Scenes use <strong>recall filters</strong> (per scene) and global safes. Save the show to USB first; practise on dLive Director.</p>", "s7-12"],
    ["what is dbfs|0 dbfs|digital meter|desk meters explained|peak vs rms|where should my meters sit", "<p>Digital desks meter in <strong>dBFS</strong>: 0 dBFS is the ceiling, so readings count down from it. Watch <strong>peak</strong> meters to avoid clipping, and leave plenty of room below 0 on the loudest moments. Nominal level is commonly −18 or −20 dBFS (lining up with +4 dBu) — check the desk's manual.</p>", "s2-12 s2-5"],
    ["how to test a cable properly|test speaker cable|test nl4|wiggle test|network cable tester", "<p>Look first, then a cable tester through every pin (each pin lights only with its partner), wiggling near each end to catch intermittents. Test all four poles of an NL4/NLT4 even if today only uses pins 1. Fails get taped, labelled and quarantined — at prep, not on site.</p>", "s3-13 s3-11"],
    ["which mic should i use|mic for each source|mic cheat sheet|best mic for vocals|which mic for kick", "<p>Loud vocals: dynamic or handheld condenser, cardioid/supercardioid. Lectern: gooseneck condenser. Moving presenter: headset or lav. Kick: kick-specific dynamic. Snare, toms, guitar cab: dynamic instrument mic. Overheads, acoustic, choir: small condensers. Bass, keys, laptops: DI. §4.9 has the table.</p>", "s4-9 s4-5"],
    ["hand over to the engineer|what to tell the engineer|engineer arrives|handover", "<p>In two minutes: what's built (and what differs from the plan), what's proved (system test, line check), what's outstanding by name, anything you changed and why, and where the spares, fault kit and show file are. Then stay close and don't change anything without asking.</p>", "s11-10"],
    ["follow the signal|pink noise test|use the oscillator to find a fault|tone test", "<p>Send pink noise at low level from the desk to one output, then follow it: desk output meter → network/link (Dante green, AES lock) → amp input indicator → amp output → cable and box. The first place it disappears is the fault. Warn people first and never send tone to IEMs.</p>", "s12-19 s12-1"],
    ["safe iem level|iems too loud|in ear hearing damage|iem limiter", "<p>Start packs low and let the performer bring them up; a good seal and a stereo mix both need less level; use limiters on IEM mixes or transmitters; add an ambient mic; and never send tone to a pack someone's wearing.</p>", "s15-13 s15-3"],
    ["mixing speech|how to mix a presenter|speech eq|eq for speech|compress speech", "<p>Gain at their real speaking level, high-pass around 100–150 Hz, cut mud or harshness rather than boosting, gentle compression (about 3:1), mics close and behind the PA, an automixer for panels, and a level comfortably above the room noise — not loud.</p>", "s16-11 s10-11"],
    ["stream audio|how loud should a stream be|stream loudness|lufs for youtube|stream mix", "<p>Build a separate stream mix, agree a loudness target with the stream team (many aim around −16 to −14 LUFS), put a limiter on the output, put any lip-sync delay on the stream audio (never the PA), use a mix-minus for remote guests, and listen to the actual stream on another device.</p>", "s14-14 s14-8"],
    ["which checklist|checklists|what checklists are there|list of checklists", "<p>Part 19 has tickable checklists for every stage: prep, load-out, load-in, line check, show day, derig, RF, advancing, monitors, recording, outdoor, access, your bag, corporate, theatre, network &amp; Dante, any desk, comms &amp; playback, fault kit, festival changeovers and end-of-job handover.</p>", "s19-1 s19-21"],
    ["network checklist|dante checklist|before trusting the network", "<p>Network drawing in hand, the right switches (EEE off, QoS set for Dante; AVB-capable for Milan), primary and secondary physically separate, every device seen on one sample rate, exactly one clock leader, all subscriptions green, and a routing preset saved.</p>", "s19-20 s8-9"],
    ["fault kit checklist|what spares to pack|spares list", "<p>Spare XLRs, 4-core NL4s and a coupler, DIs, a spare vocal mic and radio mic (synced), batteries, adapters and a labelled phase-reverse, cable tester, multimeter, torch, tape, a spare network cable — and this app offline.</p>", "s19-23 s12-2"]
  ];